var e=JSON.parse(`[[701,"The use of string concatenation in SQL queries is unsafe because it lets an attacker inject malicious SQL code through user input. That is called SQL injection. Instead you should use parameterised queries or prepared statements, which separate the data from the code and escape special characters automatically.","The problem of string concatenation\\nWhen you form an SQL query by gluing strings together, you mix the code of the query with data received from the user. If the user enters special characters such as quotation marks, they can change the logic of the query. Instead of a simple search for a name, for example, they can carry out the deletion of data or gain access to other people's records.\\nAn example of the vulnerability\\nLet us look at code in Python using the sqlite3 library:\\n\\nAs a result the query will become: \`SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '' OR '1'='1'\`. The condition \`OR '1'='1'\` is always true, so every user will be returned. That is the simplest SQL injection.\\nThe safe solution: parameterised queries\\nParameterised queries (prepared statements) pass the data separately from the code of the query. The database handles it as data rather than as part of the SQL command. An example:\\n\\nNow even if the user enters a malicious string, it will be perceived as a literal rather than as part of the SQL. The characters are escaped automatically.\\nWhere this is applied\\nParameterised queries are supported by every modern database and programming language: Python (sqlite3, psycopg2), Java (JDBC PreparedStatement), C# (SqlCommand with parameters), PHP (PDO), JavaScript (node-postgres) and others.\\nConclusion\\nThe use of string concatenation in SQL queries is categorically not recommended because of the risk of SQL injections. Always apply parameterised queries or an ORM, which escape the input automatically. That protects the data and the integrity of the database.","user_input = \\"' OR '1'='1\\"\\nquery = \\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '\\" + user_input + \\"'\\"\\ncursor.execute(query)\\n\\nuser_input = \\"' OR '1'='1\\"\\nquery = \\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?\\"\\ncursor.execute(query, (user_input,))"],[702,"JavaScript does not support multiple inheritance directly, which means a class cannot inherit properties and methods from several classes at once. Instead, JavaScript offers the use of mixins and the composition of objects to achieve a similar effect. Mixins let you add functionality from other objects without creating complex hierarchies of classes.","Multiple inheritance is the concept that lets a class inherit properties and methods from more than one parent class. Although that can be useful, many programming languages, JavaScript included, do not support multiple inheritance because of the complexity and confusion it can cause in hierarchies of classes. In JavaScript the main method of working with multiple inheritance is the use of mixins.\\nMixins: Mixins let you add functionality from other classes or objects.\\n\\nIn this example \`Dog\` inherits properties from \`Animal\` and also receives the \`run\` and \`swim\` methods through mixins. That lets you combine functionality from different sources without creating complex hierarchies of classes.\\nThe composition of objects: Instead of multiple inheritance you can create objects that contain other objects, thus combining their functionality.\\n\\nInstead of multiple inheritance, JavaScript thus offers the use of mixins and the composition of objects, which lets you manage functionality more flexibly and avoid the problems connected with classical hierarchies.","const CanRun = {\\n  run() {     \\n    console.log(\`\${this.name} is running.\`);    \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst CanSwim = {\\n  swim() {     \\n    console.log(\`\${this.name} is swimming.\`);    \\n  } \\n}; \\nclass Animal {\\n  constructor(name) {     \\n    this.name = name;    \\n  } \\n} \\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  constructor(name) {      \\n    super(name);    \\n  } \\n} \\nObject.assign(Dog.prototype, CanRun, CanSwim); \\nconst dog = new Dog('Buddy'); \\ndog.run(); // Buddy is running. \\ndog.swim(); // Buddy is swimming.\\n\\nclass Bird {\\n  fly() {    \\n    console.log('Flying!');    \\n  } \\n} \\nclass Fish {\\n  swim() {     \\n    console.log('Swimming!');    \\n  } \\n} \\nclass Duck {\\n  constructor() {    \\n    this.bird = new Bird();        \\n    this.fish = new Fish();    \\n  }    \\n  quack() {      \\n    console.log('Quack!');    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst duck = new Duck(); \\nduck.bird.fly(); // Flying! \\nduck.fish.swim(); // Swimming! \\nduck.quack(); // Quack!"],[703,"The crawling of a web application is the automated process of traversing every available page, link and form on a site. A vulnerability scanner uses crawling to compile a map of the application, in order then to check every element found for the presence of vulnerabilities. That lets you discover hidden pages, parameters and points of data entry.","What is the crawling of a web application?\\nThe crawling of a web application in the context of a vulnerability scanner is the process of automatically traversing all of an application's available resources: pages, links, forms, API endpoints and other elements. The scanner begins at one or several starting points (the main page, for example) and recursively follows every link found, gathering information about the structure of the application.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe scanner uses HTTP requests to obtain HTML pages, analyses their content, extracts every link (the \`<a>\`, \`<form>\`, \`<script>\` tags and others) and adds them to a queue for further traversal. This process continues until every available URL has been handled or the limit of the traversal depth has been reached.\\nAn example of the code of a simple crawler in Python\\n\\nWhy does a vulnerability scanner need this?\\nCrawling lets the scanner compile a complete map of the application, including hidden pages, parameters in URLs, forms for entering data and points of entry. Without crawling the scanner would not be able to find every potential target for attacks, such as XSS, SQL injections or CSRF. If the site has a search form, for example, the crawler will find it and pass it to the scanner for testing.\\nConclusion\\nCrawling is a fundamental stage of the work of any vulnerability scanner, letting it discover all of an application's available resources automatically. Without it the scanner would be limited only to the URLs explicitly given, which reduces the effectiveness of the search for vulnerabilities.","import requests\\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\\nfrom urllib.parse import urljoin\\n\\nvisited = set()\\nqueue = [\\"https://example.com\\"]\\n\\nwhile queue:\\n    url = queue.pop(0)\\n    if url in visited:\\n        continue\\n    visited.add(url)\\n    try:\\n        response = requests.get(url, timeout=5)\\n        soup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, 'html.parser')\\n        for link in soup.find_all('a'):\\n            href = link.get('href')\\n            if href:\\n                full_url = urljoin(url, href)\\n                if full_url not in visited:\\n                    queue.append(full_url)\\n    except Exception as e:\\n        print(f\\"Error: {e}\\")"],[704,"The parsing of a URL is the breaking up of an address into its constituent parts: the protocol, the domain, the port, the path, the parameters and the anchor. That is needed for extracting data from the address bar, for navigation and for handling requests. In JavaScript, for example, the URL object lets you obtain these components easily.","What is the parsing of a URL?\\nThe parsing of a URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the process of breaking up the string of an address into logical components, such as the protocol, the host, the port, the path, the query parameters and the fragment. It is a fundamental operation in web development, since browsers and servers use URLs to identify resources and to pass data.\\nThe main components of a URL\\n\\n- The protocol (scheme) — http, https, ftp, for example.\\n- The host — the domain name or the IP address.\\n- The port — the port number (by default 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).\\n- The path — the path to the resource on the server.\\n- The query parameters (query string) — a set of key=value pairs, beginning with ?.\\n- The fragment — the part after #, used for anchors on the page.\\nAn example of parsing in JavaScript\\n\\nWhat is the parsing of a URL for?\\nThe parsing of a URL is used for:\\n\\n- Extracting data from the address bar (the search parameters, for example).\\n- The validation and normalisation of a URL before sending a request.\\n- Navigation in single-page applications (SPAs) by means of React Router or Vue Router.\\n- Creating links with dynamic parameters.\\nConclusion: The parsing of a URL is a basic technique, necessary for working with web addresses. It is applied in client and server applications to handle requests, for routing and for passing data. An understanding of the structure of a URL helps you avoid errors when working with HTTP and improves the security of applications.","const url = new URL('https://example.com:8080/path/page?name=John&age=30#section');\\nconsole.log(url.protocol); // 'https:'\\nconsole.log(url.hostname); // 'example.com'\\nconsole.log(url.port); // '8080'\\nconsole.log(url.pathname); // '/path/page'\\nconsole.log(url.search); // '?name=John&age=30'\\nconsole.log(url.hash); // '#section'\\n// Obtaining the parameters\\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.get('name')); // 'John'"],[705,"MD5 suffers from serious vulnerabilities, the main one of which is collisions, where two different sets of input data give the same hash. That makes it unsuitable for cryptographic purposes such as signatures or the storage of passwords. The algorithm is also subject to preimage and second-preimage attacks, although they are less practical. Because of the high speed of the computations, MD5 easily succumbs to brute force.","The problems of the MD5 hashing algorithm\\nMD5 (Message Digest 5) is a cryptographic hash function developed in 1991. Over time serious shortcomings were discovered in it, which make it insecure for most modern applications. The main problem is the vulnerability to collisions, where two different sets of data produce the same hash.\\nCollisions\\nIn 2004 researchers demonstrated practical collisions for MD5. That means an attacker can create two different files or messages with the same hash, which breaks the integrity of the data. You can forge a digital signature or a certificate, for example.\\n\\nSpeed and brute force\\nMD5 is very fast, which makes it vulnerable to brute-force attacks. Modern hardware can compute billions of hashes per second, which lets you easily guess passwords or data. For the storage of passwords it is recommended to use slow algorithms such as bcrypt or Argon2.\\nPreimage attacks\\nAlthough preimage attacks (finding the original data from the hash) are less practical for MD5 than collisions, they do nevertheless exist. In 2009 an attack with a complexity of 2^123 was shown, which is below the theoretical limit of 2^128. That makes MD5 weaker than an ideal hash function.\\nApplication and conclusion\\nMD5 is still used to check the integrity of data in insecure environments (for the checksums of files, for example), but not for cryptographic purposes. Conclusion: MD5 should not be applied to protect passwords, digital signatures or certificates. Instead use SHA-256 or SHA-3 for hashing, and for passwords, specialised functions with a salt.","// An example of an MD5 collision (simplified)\\n// Two different messages with the same hash\\nmessage1 = \\"d131dd02c5e6eec4\\"\\nmessage2 = \\"d131dd02c5e6eec5\\"\\n// Both give the MD5: 79054025255fb1a26e4bc422aef54eb4"],[706,"XSS (cross-site scripting) is an attack in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a web page. The main ways of protecting: escaping the output of data, the validation and sanitisation of input, the use of a Content Security Policy (CSP), setting the HttpOnly flag for cookies. It also matters to avoid dangerous functions such as innerHTML and to use safe template engines.","What is XSS and why does it matter?\\nXSS (Cross-Site Scripting) is a type of attack in which an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into a web page, which is then carried out in the victim's browser. That can lead to the theft of cookies, redirection to phishing sites or a change in the content of the page. Protection against XSS is critical for any web application working with user data.\\nThe main methods of protection\\n\\n- Output escaping: The conversion of special characters (\`<\`, \`>\`, \`&\`, for example) into their HTML entities before insertion into the page. That prevents the data from being interpreted as code.\\n- The validation and sanitisation of input: The checking and cleaning of data from the user on the server. The removal of \`<script>\` tags or the use of a white list of permitted tags, for example.\\n- A Content Security Policy (CSP): An HTTP header that restricts the sources of scripts, styles and other resources. \`Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'\`, for example, permits the execution only of scripts from the same domain.\\n- The HttpOnly flag for cookies: Setting the \`HttpOnly\` flag when creating cookies prevents access to them through JavaScript, which protects against the theft of session data.\\n- Avoiding dangerous functions: Do not use \`innerHTML\`, \`document.write()\` or \`eval()\` with user data. Instead apply \`textContent\` or safe template engines (React escapes output automatically, for example).\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nProtection against XSS requires a comprehensive approach: escaping output, validating input, the use of a CSP and of safe APIs. These methods are especially important when developing applications with user content (forms, comments, chats). The application of these practices reduces the risk of attacks and raises users' trust.","// Dangerous code (vulnerable to XSS)\\nconst userInput = \\"<script>alert('XSS')<\/script>\\";\\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = userInput;\\n\\n// Safe code (escaping)\\nconst safeOutput = userInput.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;');\\ndocument.getElementById('output').textContent = safeOutput;\\n\\n// An example of a CSP in HTML\\n// <meta http-equiv=\\"Content-Security-Policy\\" content=\\"script-src 'self'\\">"],[707,"MD5 is an obsolete hash function that does not provide cryptographic strength. It is subject to collisions (two different passwords can give the same hash) and is very fast, which lets attackers guess passwords easily. Besides that, without a salt (a unique addition) identical passwords give identical hashes, which makes them vulnerable to rainbow tables. To store passwords you have to use slow hash functions with a salt — bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2, for example.","Why is MD5 unsuitable for storing passwords?\\nMD5 (Message Digest 5) is a cryptographic hash function developed in 1991. It was created to check the integrity of data rather than for the secure storage of passwords. The main problems of MD5 in the context of passwords:\\n\\n- Speed: MD5 is very fast — modern GPUs can compute billions of hashes per second. That makes brute-force and dictionary attacks trivial.\\n- Collisions: As early as 2004 practical collisions were found — two different messages giving the same hash. For passwords that means an attacker can find another password that will give the same hash and log into the system.\\n- The absence of a salt: Without a salt (a unique random string added to every password) identical passwords give identical hashes. That lets rainbow tables (precomputed hash tables) be used to find a password from a hash instantly.\\nAn example of the vulnerability\\nSuppose two users chose the password \\"password123\\". Without a salt their MD5 hashes will be identical: \`482c811da5d5b4bc6d497ffa98491e38\`. If an attacker learns the hash of one, they immediately know the password for both. With a salt every hash is unique, even if the passwords coincide.\\nHow should passwords be stored?\\nThe modern recommendations:\\n\\n- Use slow hash functions: bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 (the winner of the Password Hashing Competition).\\n- Always add a unique salt for every password (usually generated randomly and held together with the hash).\\n- Configure the cost factor in order to slow down the computation of the hash (bcrypt with cost=12 takes ~250 ms on a CPU, for example).\\nAn example of code in Node.js with bcrypt\\n\\nConclusion: MD5 is not intended for the storage of passwords because of its high speed, its vulnerability to collisions and the absence of built-in support for a salt. To protect user data always use specialised algorithms for hashing passwords, such as bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2, with the mandatory addition of a salt and the configuration of the cost factor.","const bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\\nconst saltRounds = 12;\\n\\nasync function hashPassword(password) {\\n  const salt = await bcrypt.genSalt(saltRounds);\\n  const hash = await bcrypt.hash(password, salt);\\n  return hash; // we save it in the database\\n}\\n\\nasync function checkPassword(password, hash) {\\n  return await bcrypt.compare(password, hash);\\n}"],[708,"The ternary operator is justified when you have to assign a value to a variable depending on a condition, and it makes the code shorter and more understandable. It suits simple conditions with two variants. It is not worth using it for complex nested conditions or when the result is not assigned to a variable.","When to use the ternary operator\\nThe ternary operator (\`condition ? value1 : value2\`) is a compact alternative to the \`if...else\` construction. Its main aim is to make code more concise when you have to choose one of two values on the basis of a condition.\\nAn example of justified use\\nIt is best to apply the ternary operator to assign a value to a variable:\\n\\nThis code reads easily: if the age is greater than or equal to 18, then the status is 'Adult', otherwise 'Minor'.\\nWhen it is not worth using\\n\\n- For nested conditions — that worsens readability.\\n- When the result is not assigned to a variable but used for side effects (calling functions, for example).\\n- If the condition is complex or long.\\nConclusion\\nThe ternary operator is justified for simple, one-line assignments, where it improves the readability of the code. In the remaining cases it is better to use \`if...else\` or \`switch\`.","const age = 20;\\nconst status = age >= 18 ? 'Adult' : 'Minor';\\nconsole.log(status); // 'Adult'"],[709,"Passing objects by reference can lead to the unintended change of data in different parts of a program. That happens when a function changes an object passed as an argument and those changes are reflected in the original. Such side effects make debugging more complex and can cause bugs, especially in large projects. To avoid problems it is recommended to create copies of objects before changing them.","The problems of passing by reference\\nIn JavaScript objects (arrays and functions included) are passed by reference rather than by value. That means that on assignment or when passing into a function, what is copied is not the object itself but the reference to it. Changing the object through one reference affects every other reference pointing to the same object.\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Unintended mutations: A function can change the object passed, which will affect other parts of the code using that object.\\n- The complexity of debugging: Side effects make the code less predictable, especially in asynchronous operations or when working with state.\\n- Problems with state: In React or Redux, mutating objects directly breaks the principles of immutability, which can lead to rendering errors.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of passing by reference is critical for writing reliable code. Use immutable approaches (the spread operator, Object.assign, libraries of the Immer kind) to prevent side effects, especially in functional programming and the management of state.","const user = { name: 'Alice', age: 25 };\\n\\nfunction updateAge(person) {\\n  person.age = 30; // A mutation of the original\\n}\\n\\nupdateAge(user);\\nconsole.log(user.age); // 30 — the original has been changed\\n\\n// How to avoid it: we create a copy\\nfunction safeUpdateAge(person) {\\n  return { ...person, age: 30 };\\n}\\n\\nconst updatedUser = safeUpdateAge(user);\\nconsole.log(user.age); // 25 — the original has not been touched"],[710,"GET requests have a restriction on the length of the URL (usually 2048 characters), should not contain a request body, are cached by browsers and proxies, and are idempotent. They are unsuitable for passing confidential data, since the parameters are visible in the URL.","The restrictions of GET requests\\nGET requests are the main HTTP method for obtaining data from a server. They have several important restrictions, however, which developers have to take into account when designing APIs and web applications.\\nThe length of the URL\\nThe best-known restriction is the maximum length of the URL. Although the HTTP specification establishes no strict limit, browsers and servers usually restrict a URL to 2048 characters. Internet Explorer, for example, supports up to 2083 characters, while Apache supports up to 8190. That means passing a large quantity of data through query parameters can lead to the request being truncated or to an error.\\nThe absence of a request body\\nGET requests should not contain a body. Although some clients can send a body, servers often ignore it or return an error. All of the data is therefore passed only through the URL (query parameters or the path).\\nCaching\\nGET requests are cached by default by browsers and proxy servers. That can be useful for raising performance, but it can also lead to stale data being served. To control caching the Cache-Control, Expires and ETag headers are used.\\nIdempotency and safety\\nGET requests are idempotent — carrying out one and the same request many times should not change the state of the server. They are also considered safe, since they are not intended to change data. That means GET should not be used for operations that have side effects (the deletion or creation of resources, for example).\\nThe security of the data\\nThe parameters of a GET request are visible in the URL, which makes them unsafe for passing confidential information (passwords, tokens, personal data). URLs can be saved in the browser's history and in the server's logs, and passed in the Referer header.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nGET requests suit obtaining data when the parameters do not exceed the URL limit and do not contain confidential information. To pass large volumes of data or to change the state of the server, POST or other methods should be used.","// An example of a GET request with a large number of parameters\\nfetch('/api/users?name=John&age=30&city=NewYork&country=USA&limit=100&offset=0')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));\\n\\n// If there are too many parameters, it is better to use POST\\nconst params = new URLSearchParams({ name: 'John', age: 30, city: 'New York' });\\nif (params.toString().length > 2048) {\\n  // We use POST instead of GET\\n}"],[711,"async and await are syntactic sugar over promises. async makes a function asynchronous and forces it to return a promise. await suspends the execution of the function until the promise resolves, making the code resemble synchronous code. That makes reading and debugging asynchronous code simpler.","The main concept\\nasync and await are the modern way of working with asynchronous code in JavaScript, introduced in ES2017. They do not replace promises but make using them more convenient and readable. The async keyword before a function guarantees that it will return a promise, even if there is no explicit return inside. await can be used only inside async functions; it suspends the execution until the promise has finished and returns its result.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nasync/await is widely used in web development to handle requests to APIs, to read files, to work with databases and for any other operations requiring waiting. It is especially useful in scenarios where several asynchronous operations have to be carried out one after another, or in parallel by means of Promise.all.\\nConclusion\\nasync/await is worth applying everywhere asynchrony is required, in order to make the code more linear and understandable, especially when working with chains of promises or with complex logic of dependencies.","// Without async/await\\nfunction fetchData() {\\n  return fetch('https://api.example.com/data')\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .then(data => console.log(data));\\n}\\n\\n// With async/await\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\\n  const data = await response.json();\\n  console.log(data);\\n}"],[712,"Copying data structures during recursion prevents the accidental change of the original data. Without a copy, every call can mutate the shared object, which leads to unpredictable results. That is especially important in functional programming, where data has to be immutable. A copy guarantees that every recursive step works with its own version of the data.","Why copy data during recursion?\\nRecursive functions often work with one and the same data structures at different levels of the call. If a copy is not created, the changes brought in during one call will affect the other calls, which will break the logic of the algorithm. That is called a side effect.\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a function that deletes an element from an array recursively:\\n\\nHere \`arr.splice\` changes the original array, which can cause errors in the calling code.\\nThe correct approach\\nCreate a copy before changing it:\\n\\nNow every call works with a new copy, and the original array stays untouched.\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\n- Functional programming (React, Redux)\\n- Algorithms on graphs and trees\\n- Processing data without mutation\\nConclusion: Copying data during recursion is a basic practice for writing predictable, safe code, especially in immutable architectures.","function removeItem(arr, item) {\\n  const index = arr.indexOf(item);\\n  if (index === -1) return arr;\\n  arr.splice(index, 1); // a mutation of the original array\\n  return removeItem(arr, item);\\n}\\n\\nfunction removeItemSafe(arr, item) {\\n  const index = arr.indexOf(item);\\n  if (index === -1) return arr;\\n  const newArr = [...arr]; // a copy\\n  newArr.splice(index, 1);\\n  return removeItemSafe(newArr, item);\\n}"],[713,"Modules in JavaScript are a way of structuring code that lets you divide it into separate parts. Every module can contain functions, classes and variables, which can be exported and imported into other modules. That helps organise the code, manage dependencies and reduce the likelihood of name conflicts.","Modules in JavaScript are a mechanism that lets you divide code into separate, isolated parts. That is especially useful for organising large applications, where a multitude of functions and classes can lead to confusion. Modules help prevent name conflicts and manage the dependencies between the various parts of an application.\\n\\n- Creating a module: To create a module you can use the \`export\` and \`import\` keywords.\\n\\n- Importing a module: Modules can be imported into another file.\\n\\n- The default export: You can also export one object by default from a module.\\n\\n- The advantages of modules: The use of modules helps organise code, improves readability, lets you reuse functionality and manage dependencies.\\n\\nModules are becoming ever more popular in modern web applications and are the foundation for working with tools such as Webpack and Babel, which help manage dependencies and optimise code.","// math.js (the module) \\nexport function add(x, y) {\\n  return x + y; \\n} \\nexport function subtract(x, y) {\\n  return x - y; \\n}\\n\\n// app.js \\nimport { add, subtract } from './math.js'; \\nconsole.log(add(5, 3)); // 8 \\nconsole.log(subtract(5, 3)); // 2\\n\\n// car.js \\nexport default class Car {\\n  constructor(brand) {   \\n    this.brand = brand;    \\n  }    \\n  drive() {    \\n    console.log(\`Driving a \${this.brand}\`);    \\n  } \\n}\\n\\n// app.js \\nimport Car from './car.js'; \\nconst myCar = new Car('Toyota'); \\nmyCar.drive(); // Driving a Toyota"],[714,"To avoid the duplication of combinations, you can use a set (Set) to hold the unique results, or sort the elements before adding them. When generating all of the subsets of an array, for example, sort it and skip repeating elements at every step. That guarantees that every combination will be unique.","The problem of the duplication of combinations\\nWhen solving tasks such as the generation of all of the subsets or permutations, the problem of duplication often arises, where one and the same combinations appear several times because of repeating elements in the input data. That can lead to incorrect results or to superfluous computations.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- The use of a set (Set): Hold every combination generated in a set, which deletes duplicates automatically. In Python, for example, you can convert a list of combinations into a set, if they are hashable.\\n- Sorting and skipping duplicates: Sort the input array and, during the recursive traversal, skip the elements that are equal to the previous one and were not used at the current level. That is effective for tasks with backtracking.\\nAn example of code in Python\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe application of sorting and the skipping of duplicates is the standard, effective way of avoiding the duplication of combinations in backtracking algorithms. This approach is widely used in interview tasks and when working with combinatorial data structures.","def unique_combinations(nums):\\n    nums.sort()\\n    result = []\\n    def backtrack(start, path):\\n        result.append(path[:])\\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\\n            if i > start and nums[i] == nums[i-1]:\\n                continue\\n            path.append(nums[i])\\n            backtrack(i + 1, path)\\n            path.pop()\\n    backtrack(0, [])\\n    return result\\n\\nprint(unique_combinations([1, 2, 2]))\\n# [[], [1], [1,2], [1,2,2], [2], [2,2]]"],[715,"The optimisation of brute force includes pruning, memoisation, the use of dynamic programming and heuristics. In the knapsack problem, for example, branch and bound is applied. That reduces the number of variants checked and speeds up the search for a solution.","The main ways of optimising brute-force algorithms\\nGoing through every possible variant (brute force) often leads to exponential complexity. To speed it up, several key techniques are applied, which shorten the search space or avoid repeated computations.\\nPruning\\nThe idea consists in discarding variants that are obviously unpromising at the early stages. In the travelling-salesman problem, for example, you can avoid considering routes whose length already exceeds the current best result.\\n\\nMemoisation and dynamic programming\\nIf subtasks repeat, their results are saved. In the knapsack problem with repeating weights, for example, a DP table is used.\\n\\nHeuristics and approximate methods\\nFor NP-hard problems, greedy algorithms or genetic algorithms are applied, which give a good but not guaranteed optimal solution in a reasonable time.\\nConclusion: The optimisation of brute force is necessary in tasks with a large search space, such as combinatorial optimisation, planning and routing. The choice of method depends on the structure of the task and the precision required.","def tsp(graph, visited, current, count, cost, best):\\n    if count == len(graph) and graph[current][0]:\\n        best = min(best, cost + graph[current][0])\\n        return best\\n    for next in range(len(graph)):\\n        if not visited[next] and graph[current][next]:\\n            if cost + graph[current][next] < best:  # pruning\\n                visited[next] = True\\n                best = tsp(graph, visited, next, count+1, cost+graph[current][next], best)\\n                visited[next] = False\\n    return best\\n\\ndef knapsack(weights, values, W):\\n    dp = [0] * (W+1)\\n    for i in range(len(weights)):\\n        for w in range(W, weights[i]-1, -1):\\n            dp[w] = max(dp[w], dp[w-weights[i]] + values[i])\\n    return dp[W]"],[716,"To search for combinations in an array, recursive backtracking is often used. This approach lets you generate every possible combination while pruning unpromising branches. Iterative methods using bit masks are also applied for small arrays. The choice depends on the size of the data and on the performance requirements.","The search for combinations in an array is a classic task, which tests the ability to work with recursion and the optimisation of brute force. The main difficulty consists in the fact that the number of combinations grows exponentially, so it matters to choose an effective approach.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Backtracking (recursive brute force with return) — the most universal method. It builds combinations recursively, adding elements one at a time, and rolls back when the combination is complete or cannot be continued. That lets you avoid holding every combination in memory.\\n- The iterative method with bit masks — suits small arrays (up to 20-30 elements). Every combination is represented by a binary number, where a bit indicates the inclusion of an element. Simple to implement, but ineffective for large data.\\n- Dynamic programming — used when you have to find combinations with a particular sum or property — the knapsack problem, for example. It lets you shorten the brute force by remembering intermediate results.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript (backtracking)\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The generation of all of the subsets for the analysis of data.\\n- Solving interview tasks (combinations of a sum, for example).\\n- The search for optimal solutions in combinatorial tasks.\\nConclusion: Backtracking is the main tool for tasks on combinations, since it is flexible and effective for most cases. Iterative methods with bit masks are worth using only for small arrays, and dynamic programming when there are additional restrictions — the sum of the elements, for example.","function getCombinations(arr, k) {\\n  const result = [];\\n  function backtrack(start, current) {\\n    if (current.length === k) {\\n      result.push([...current]);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    for (let i = start; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n      current.push(arr[i]);\\n      backtrack(i + 1, current);\\n      current.pop(); // the rollback\\n    }\\n  }\\n  backtrack(0, []);\\n  return result;\\n}\\nconsole.log(getCombinations([1,2,3], 2)); // [[1,2],[1,3],[2,3]]"],[717,"To handle millions of records on the front end you cannot load all of the data at once. Use server-side pagination with a limit and offset/cursor, in order to pass the data in portions. For the display, apply virtual scrolling (react-window, for example), which renders only the visible elements. You can also use lazy loading and the chunking of the data on the server.","The problem of loading large data\\nWhen millions of records have to be served to the front end, passing the whole array directly will lead to the browser's memory overflowing, the interface hanging and an enormous loading time. Techniques are therefore applied that minimise the volume of data passed and optimise the rendering.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Server-side pagination: the server returns only one page of data (50 records, for example). The client requests the next page by offset or cursor. That reduces the load on the network and on memory.\\n- Virtual scrolling: libraries of the react-window or react-virtualized kind render only those DOM elements that are visible in the browser's window. The remaining records exist only in the data, not in the DOM.\\n- Lazy loading: the data is loaded as the user scrolls (infinite scroll) or on the user's request (a \\"Load more\\" button).\\nAn example of the implementation of pagination on the server (Node.js + Express)\\n\\nAn example of virtual scrolling in React\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo work with millions of records on the front end, be sure to combine server-side pagination with virtual scrolling. That will ensure fast loading, low memory consumption and smooth scrolling. This approach is applied in tables, lists of messages, logs and any interfaces with large sets of data.","app.get('/api/users', async (req, res) => {\\n  const { page = 1, limit = 50 } = req.query;\\n  const offset = (page - 1) * limit;\\n  const users = await db.query(\\n    'SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY id LIMIT $1 OFFSET $2',\\n    [limit, offset]\\n  );\\n  res.json({ data: users, total: 1000000 });\\n});\\n\\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n  <div style={style}>Row {index}</div>\\n);\\n\\nconst BigList = () => (\\n  <List height={600} itemCount={1000000} itemSize={35} width=\\"100%\\">\\n    {Row}\\n  </List>\\n);"],[718,"An adapter is a structural pattern that lets objects with incompatible interfaces work together. It wraps one object, providing another interface, the one the client expects. That is useful when integrating legacy systems or third-party libraries without changing their code.","What is the Adapter pattern?\\nThe Adapter pattern is a structural design pattern that lets objects with incompatible interfaces interact. It acts as an intermediate layer, converting the calls of one interface into the calls of another. That is especially useful when integrating systems, when you cannot or do not want to change the existing code.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe adapter contains a reference to the object being adapted and implements the target interface. When the client calls a method of the adapter, it delegates the execution to the object being adapted, possibly with a conversion of the data or of the format of the call.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Integration with third-party APIs or libraries.\\n- The connection of legacy systems to new modules.\\n- Ensuring compatibility between different versions of interfaces.\\nConclusion: The Adapter pattern is worth applying when systems with different interfaces have to be joined without changing their source code. It makes integration simpler and raises the flexibility of the architecture.","// The target interface the client expects\\nclass Target {\\n  request() {\\n    return 'Target: Default behavior';\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The class being adapted, with an incompatible interface\\nclass Adaptee {\\n  specificRequest() {\\n    return 'Adaptee: Specific behavior';\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The adapter\\nclass Adapter extends Target {\\n  constructor(adaptee) {\\n    super();\\n    this.adaptee = adaptee;\\n  }\\n\\n  request() {\\n    const result = this.adaptee.specificRequest();\\n    return \`Adapter: (TRANSLATED) \${result}\`;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst adaptee = new Adaptee();\\nconst adapter = new Adapter(adaptee);\\nconsole.log(adapter.request()); // Adapter: (TRANSLATED) Adaptee: Specific behavior"],[719,"A process is an isolated environment for the execution of a program with its own memory. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution inside a process, sharing its memory. Asynchrony is an approach to organising the non-blocking execution of tasks, often within a single thread, by means of an event loop. Processes are isolated, threads can communicate through shared memory, and asynchrony lets you handle I/O operations effectively without creating additional threads.","The main concepts\\nA process, a thread and asynchrony are three different approaches to carrying out several tasks simultaneously or pseudo-simultaneously. A process represents a running program with its own address space, memory and resources. A thread is a more lightweight unit of execution inside a process, which shares memory and resources with the other threads of the same process. Asynchrony is a programming model that lets you carry out tasks without blocking the main thread, using mechanisms of the event-loop and callback kind.\\nThe differences and examples\\nProcesses are isolated from one another, which ensures security but requires inter-process communication (IPC) to exchange data. Threads can access shared memory directly, which makes the exchange of data simpler but requires synchronisation (mutexes, for example) to avoid races. Asynchrony often works in a single thread, switching between tasks while waiting for I/O, which reduces the overheads of creating threads.\\n\\nApplication\\nProcesses are used for isolation and to distribute the load between CPU cores. Threads suit tasks requiring shared state and a frequent exchange of data. Asynchrony is ideal for I/O-intensive operations (network requests, work with files) in single-threaded applications, such as web servers on Node.js.\\nConclusion: The choice between processes, threads and asynchrony depends on the task: for CPU-intensive computations use processes, for shared state threads, for I/O operations asynchrony.","// An example of asynchrony in JavaScript\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com');\\n  const data = await response.json();\\n  console.log(data);\\n}\\n\\n// An example of threads in Python\\nimport threading\\n\\ndef worker():\\n    print('Thread working')\\n\\nthread = threading.Thread(target=worker)\\nthread.start()\\nthread.join()\\n\\n// An example of processes in Python\\nimport multiprocessing\\n\\ndef worker():\\n    print('Process working')\\n\\nprocess = multiprocessing.Process(target=worker)\\nprocess.start()\\nprocess.join()"],[720,"To test a front end without a back end, mocks and stubs are used. Mocks imitate the server's responses, letting you check the behaviour of the interface. That speeds up development and isolates the tests from external dependencies.","What are mocks and stubs?\\nMocks and stubs are test doubles that replace real dependencies such as a backend API. They let you imitate the server's responses, network errors or delays without requiring a working server.\\nHow does it work?\\nInstead of a real HTTP request to the back end, the test intercepts the call and returns data given in advance. By means of the \`jest\` and \`msw\` (Mock Service Worker) libraries, for example, you can intercept requests at the level of the network.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- The development of a front end in parallel with a back end.\\n- Testing the handling of errors (a 500 error, for example).\\n- The autonomous testing of components without external dependencies.\\nConclusion: mocks and stubs are an indispensable tool for testing a front end when the back end is unavailable or unstable. They speed up development and raise the reliability of the tests.","import { rest } from 'msw';\\nimport { setupServer } from 'msw/node';\\n\\nconst server = setupServer(\\n  rest.get('/api/users', (req, res, ctx) => {\\n    return res(ctx.json([{ id: 1, name: 'John' }]));\\n  })\\n);\\n\\nbeforeAll(() => server.listen());\\nafterEach(() => server.resetHandlers());\\nafterAll(() => server.close());\\n\\ntest('displays users', async () => {\\n  render(<UserList />);\\n  expect(await screen.findByText('John')).toBeInTheDocument();\\n});"],[721,"Asynchronous processes are tested by means of callbacks, promises or async/await. In Jest you can use done() for callbacks, return a promise or use async/await. It matters to wait for the asynchronous operation to finish before checking the result.","The main approaches to testing asynchronous code\\nAsynchronous operations such as requests to an API, timers or the reading of files require a special approach in tests. Without the correct waiting, a test can finish before the check has been carried out, which will lead to false results.\\nTesting with callbacks\\nIn frameworks of the Mocha or Jest kind you can pass a done function, which is called after the asynchronous operation has finished. If done is not called, the test will fail on a timeout.\\n\\nTesting with promises\\nSimply return the promise from the test — the framework will wait for it to resolve.\\n\\nTesting with async/await\\nThe most readable way is to use async/await.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse async/await for the cleanliness of the code, promises for compatibility, and done only if you are working with callbacks. That will ensure the reliable testing of asynchronous processes.","test('async callback', (done) => {\\n  fetchData((data) => {\\n    expect(data).toBe('result');\\n    done();\\n  });\\n});\\n\\ntest('async promise', () => {\\n  return fetchData().then(data => {\\n    expect(data).toBe('result');\\n  });\\n});\\n\\ntest('async await', async () => {\\n  const data = await fetchData();\\n  expect(data).toBe('result');\\n});"],[722,"In an incident with authorisation, first check the status of the server and the logs. Then make sure the database of users is available and correct. Check the settings of the sessions and the tokens. If the problem is on the client's side, clear the cache and the cookies. In the event of errors in the code, correct them and restart the service.","The diagnosis of a problem with authorisation\\nIn an incident connected with the impossibility of authorisation, the first thing necessary is to determine at which level the error arises: on the client's side, the server's or in the network. Begin by checking the availability of the server and its logs. In a Node.js application, for example, you can look at the error logs:\\n\\nChecking the database and the sessions\\nMake sure the database of users is working and contains correct records. Check whether the sessions or the tokens have expired. When using JWT, for example:\\n\\nThe client side\\nIf the server is working, check the client part: clear the browser's cache, the cookies and localStorage. Sometimes the problem is caused by stale data. Also make sure the requests are being sent to the correct URL.\\nConclusion\\nA systematic approach to the diagnosis — checking the server, the database, the sessions and the client — lets you localise and eliminate a problem with authorisation quickly. This method is applicable in any web application using authentication.","// An example of checking the logs in Express\\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\\n  console.error('Auth error:', err.message);\\n  res.status(500).send('Internal Server Error');\\n});\\n\\n// Checking the token's period of validity\\nconst token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];\\nif (!token) return res.status(401).send('No token');\\ntry {\\n  const decoded = jwt.verify(token, secret);\\n  req.user = decoded;\\n} catch (err) {\\n  return res.status(401).send('Invalid token');\\n}"],[723,"A token is passed in an HTTP request through the Authorization header. Usually the Bearer scheme is used, where the token is stated after the word Bearer. For example: Authorization: Bearer <token>. That is the standard way for REST APIs and JWT.","How a token is passed in API requests\\nAn authentication token is passed in an HTTP request through the \`Authorization\` header. That is a standard mechanism described in the HTTP specification, which lets the server identify the client without the need to pass the login and the password on every request.\\nThe Bearer scheme\\nThe most widespread scheme is Bearer. The token is stated after the word Bearer and a space. An example:\\n\\nThis scheme is used for JWT (JSON Web Tokens) and other types of token.\\nOther schemes\\n\\n- Basic — the passing of the login and the password in Base64 encoding (obsolete, insecure).\\n- Digest — a more complex scheme with hashing, rarely used.\\n- An API Key — sometimes passed in the \`X-API-Key\` header or in query parameters.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript (fetch)\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nTokens are passed in headers when working with REST APIs, GraphQL, WebSocket (on initialisation) and in mobile applications. That is safer than passing a token in the URL (query parameters), since URLs can be logged by servers and proxies.\\nConclusion: Use the \`Authorization: Bearer <token>\` header to pass a token in API requests. That is the standard, safe and widely supported approach.","Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\\n\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data', {\\n  method: 'GET',\\n  headers: {\\n    'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + token\\n  }\\n})\\n.then(response => response.json())\\n.then(data => console.log(data));"],[724,"In JavaScript there are two types of module export: default and named. A default export lets you export one object, function or class, which will be used by default on import. A named export lets you export several objects, functions or classes, each of which has to be imported using its name.","Modules in JavaScript let you structure code and manage dependencies. There are two main types of export: default and named.\\n\\n- The default export: Lets you export one object, function or class from a module. When importing that module you can choose any name for the imported value.\\n\\nOn import:\\n\\n- The named export: Lets you export several objects, functions or classes. On import you have to use the same names as on export.\\n\\nOn import:\\n\\nDefault and named exports thus provide flexibility in the organisation and use of modules, which makes managing dependencies and the structure of the code simpler.","// math.js \\nexport default function add(x, y) {\\n    return x + y; \\n}\\n\\n// app.js \\nimport myAddFunction from './math.js'; \\nconsole.log(myAddFunction(5, 3)); // 8\\n\\n// math.js \\nexport function add(x, y) {\\n    return x + y; \\n} \\nexport function subtract(x, y) {\\n    return x - y; \\n}\\n\\n// app.js \\nimport { add, subtract } from './math.js'; \\nconsole.log(add(5, 3)); // 8 \\nconsole.log(subtract(5, 3)); // 2"],[725,"Testing the validation of user input includes checking for correctness, security and conformity to the formats expected. Unit tests are used for the individual validation functions and integration tests to check the interaction with the server. It matters to test the boundary cases, such as empty strings, special characters and SQL injections.","What is the testing of the validation of user input?\\nThe validation of user input is the process of checking the data entered by the user for conformity to particular rules. Testing this validation is necessary to prevent errors and attacks (XSS or SQL injections, for example) and to ensure the correct working of the application. It includes checking both on the client's side (the browser) and on the server's side.\\nThe main approaches to testing\\n\\n- Unit testing: Checking the individual validation functions (checking an email or the length of a string, for example).\\n- Integration testing: Checking the complete cycle of entering data, including sending it to the server and handling it.\\n- Security testing: Checking for vulnerabilities such as injections or the forging of data.\\nAn example of code for a unit test\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Test the boundary cases: empty strings, very long strings, special characters.\\n- Use automated tests for regression.\\n- Check both the client and the server validation, since the client one can be bypassed.\\nConclusion: Testing the validation of input is critically important for the security and the reliability of an application. It helps prevent errors and attacks, ensuring the correct handling of data.","// The email validation function\\nfunction validateEmail(email) {\\n  const re = /^[^\\\\s@]+@[^\\\\s@]+\\\\.[^\\\\s@]+$/;\\n  return re.test(email);\\n}\\n\\n// The test\\nconsole.assert(validateEmail('test@example.com') === true, 'Valid email');\\nconsole.assert(validateEmail('invalid') === false, 'Invalid email');"],[726,"A/B testing is a method of comparing two versions of a product (A and B) in order to determine which of them is better by the metrics given. A beta rollout is the gradual release of new functionality to a limited percentage of users in order to reduce the risks. Both approaches let you test hypotheses and gather feedback before a full release.","What are A/B testing and a beta rollout?\\nA/B testing (split testing) is an experimental method in which two groups of users see different versions of a product (the control A and the experimental B). The aim is to determine, with statistical significance, which version is better by the key metrics (conversion, engagement, revenue). A beta rollout (or canary deployment) is a strategy of gradually introducing new functionality, where the update is first received by a small part of the users (5%, for example), and then the percentage is increased in the absence of problems.\\nHow does it work in practice?\\nBoth approaches are often implemented through feature flags — conditional switches in the code that let you turn functionality on and off without a deployment. An example in JavaScript:\\n\\nFor A/B testing, server-side logic is usually used, in order to fix the user to a variant:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- A/B testing — in marketing (headlines, the colours of buttons), UX (the arrangement of elements), algorithms (ranking).\\n- A beta rollout — in CI/CD for a safe deployment, when introducing critical changes (a new recommendation algorithm, for example).\\nConclusion\\nUse A/B testing to take decisions on the basis of data, and a beta rollout to minimise the risks during a release. Both methods raise the reliability and the effectiveness of development.","// An example of the use of a feature flag for a beta rollout\\nconst isBetaEnabled = Math.random() < 0.1; // 10% of the users\\nif (isBetaEnabled) {\\n  showNewFeature();\\n} else {\\n  showOldFeature();\\n}\\n\\n// An example of an A/B test in Node.js\\napp.get('/api/experiment', (req, res) => {\\n  const userId = req.cookies.userId;\\n  const variant = getVariant(userId); // A or B\\n  res.json({ variant, data: getData(variant) });\\n});"],[727,"Chrome DevTools is a built-in set of tools for debugging web applications. It includes the console for viewing errors, the Network tab for analysing network requests, Sources for debugging JavaScript and Elements for viewing the DOM. These tools help developers find and correct errors quickly.","What is Chrome DevTools?\\nChrome DevTools is a set of tools for developers, built into the Google Chrome browser. It lets you analyse and debug web pages, reveal JavaScript errors, optimise performance and study network requests. DevTools is indispensable for front-end developers when building and maintaining web applications.\\nThe main tools for analysing errors\\n\\n- Console — displays JavaScript errors, warnings and logs. Lets you execute code in real time.\\n- Sources — provides access to the source code, lets you set breakpoints and execute code step by step.\\n- Network — shows every network request, its status, loading time and headers. Helps reveal problems with APIs or resources.\\n- Elements — lets you view and edit HTML and CSS in real time, which is useful for checking styles.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose the data is not loading on a page. Open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and refresh the page. You will see the list of requests. If the request to the API is marked in red (status 404 or 500), that points to an error on the server. In the console a message about the error with the details may appear at the same time.\\n\\nIn the DevTools console you will see the text of the error if the request failed. Using the Sources tab, you can set a breakpoint inside .catch() and check the value of error.\\nConclusion\\nChrome DevTools is the main tool for debugging web applications. A knowledge of it is mandatory for any front-end developer, since it speeds up the search for and correction of errors and also helps optimise performance.","// An example of code that can raise an error\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[728,"The analysis of network errors and HTTP codes helps you understand what went wrong during a request. 2xx codes mean success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client errors (404 — not found, for example), 5xx server errors. Network errors such as timeouts or DNS failures require a check of the connection and tools of the curl or browser-console kind.","The basics of HTTP codes and network errors\\nHTTP codes are standard responses of a server, which point to the result of handling a request. They divide into classes: 1xx (informational), 2xx (success), 3xx (redirection), 4xx (client errors) and 5xx (server errors). Network errors such as timeouts, DNS failures or a refusal of connection arise before an HTTP response is received and require analysis at the level of the network.\\nHow to analyse\\nFor the analysis use the developer tools in the browser (the Network tab) or command-line utilities, curl for example. An example of checking the response code:\\n\\nIf the code is 404, check the URL. If it is 500, the problem is on the server. Network errors such as 'ERR_CONNECTION_TIMED_OUT' point to the unavailability of the server or to blocking by a firewall.\\nA practical example\\nSuppose you make a request to an API and receive an error. Here is how to handle it in JavaScript:\\n\\nThis code checks the status of the response and catches both HTTP errors (4xx, 5xx) and network ones (the absence of internet, for example).\\nConclusion\\nThe analysis of HTTP codes and network errors is necessary for the fast diagnosis of problems in web applications. Use the developer tools and the handling of errors in the code, in order to improve the user experience and make debugging simpler.","curl -I https://example.com/api\\nHTTP/2 200\\ncontent-type: application/json\\n\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => {\\n    console.error('Network error:', error.message);\\n  });"],[729,"SQL injection is an attack in which an attacker injects malicious SQL code into queries to a database. It happens when user input is concatenated directly into an SQL query without escaping. Testing for SQL injections is necessary in order to reveal and eliminate such vulnerabilities, so as to protect data from unauthorised access.","What is SQL injection?\\nSQL injection is a type of attack on web applications in which an attacker can carry out arbitrary SQL queries to a database. It happens when an application forms SQL queries by inserting user input directly without due handling. If you pass \`' OR '1'='1\` into the login input field, for example, the query may become \`SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1'\`, which will return every user.\\nWhy test for SQL injections?\\nTesting for SQL injections is critically important for security. A successful attack can lead to the leak of confidential data (passwords, card numbers), to its change or deletion, and also to gaining full control over the server. Regular testing helps reveal vulnerabilities before attackers take advantage of them.\\nAn example of vulnerable code\\n\\nHow to protect yourself?\\n\\n- Use parameterised queries (prepared statements).\\n- Escape user input.\\n- Apply an ORM (Hibernate, Entity Framework, for example), which handles input automatically.\\nAn example of safe code\\n\\nConclusion: SQL injections are one of the most dangerous vulnerabilities of web applications. Testing and the use of parameterised queries are mandatory for any application working with databases, especially if it handles user input.","// Vulnerable code in PHP\\n$username = $_POST['username'];\\n$query = \\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '$username'\\";\\n$result = mysqli_query($conn, $query);\\n\\n// Safe code in PHP with a prepared statement\\n$stmt = $conn->prepare(\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?\\");\\n$stmt->bind_param(\\"s\\", $_POST['username']);\\n$stmt->execute();\\n$result = $stmt->get_result();"],[730,"Request interception lets you substitute responses given in advance for real HTTP requests. That is needed for testing without a dependence on external services. You can imitate different scenarios, such as server errors or delays. Tools of the Cypress or Playwright kind provide built-in methods for it.","What is request interception?\\nRequest interception is a technique in which a test framework intercepts the outgoing HTTP requests from an application and returns responses given in advance, without sending the request to a real server. That lets you test an application's behaviour in various conditions, such as successful responses, errors, timeouts or slow networks, without the need to set up a real back end.\\nWhy is it needed?\\nThe main aim is the isolation of the tests from external dependencies. Real servers can be unavailable, return unpredictable data or have restrictions on speed. Request interception makes tests faster, more reliable and deterministic. That is especially useful when testing a front end, where it matters to check how the UI reacts to different API responses.\\nAn example with Cypress\\nIn Cypress the \`cy.intercept()\` command is used for interception. Here is a simple example:\\n\\nHere we intercept the GET request to \`/api/users\` and return the data from the \`users.json\` file. Then we check that the list of users is displayed.\\nAn example with Playwright\\nIn Playwright the \`page.route()\` method is used:\\n\\nThis code intercepts every request matching the pattern and returns a fake response.\\nConclusion\\nRequest interception is a powerful tool for creating reliable, fast tests. It is worth applying in any project where there is interaction with external APIs, in order to avoid instability of the tests and to speed up their execution.","cy.intercept('GET', '/api/users', { fixture: 'users.json' }).as('getUsers');\\ncy.visit('/users');\\ncy.wait('@getUsers');\\ncy.get('.user-list').should('be.visible');\\n\\nawait page.route('**/api/users', route => {\\n  route.fulfill({\\n    status: 200,\\n    contentType: 'application/json',\\n    body: JSON.stringify([{ id: 1, name: 'John' }])\\n  });\\n});\\nawait page.goto('/users');\\nawait expect(page.locator('.user-name')).toHaveText('John');"],[731,"Negative scenarios for the entry of data include empty strings, null, undefined, NaN, very long strings, special characters (SQL injections, XSS), negative numbers, floating-point numbers, incorrect data types (a string instead of a number), and the breaking of business rules (a date in the past, for example). Testing these scenarios helps reveal vulnerabilities and errors of handling.","What are negative scenarios for the entry of data?\\nNegative scenarios are test cases that check how a system reacts to incorrect, unexpected or malicious data. They are critically important for ensuring the reliability and the security of an application.\\nThe main categories of negative scenario\\n\\n- Empty and null values: an empty string, null, undefined, NaN. They check that the system does not crash and gives an understandable error.\\n- Boundary values: very long strings (buffer overflow), negative numbers, floating-point numbers, maximum/minimum values.\\n- Special characters: SQL injections (' OR 1=1 --), XSS attacks (\`<script>alert(1)<\/script>\`), control characters.\\n- Incorrect data types: a string instead of a number, an object instead of a string, an array instead of a boolean value.\\n- The breaking of business rules: a date of birth in the future, a negative age, an email without @, a password without digits.\\nAn example of code with the handling of negative scenarios\\n\\nConclusion\\nTesting negative scenarios is mandatory for any production application. It prevents vulnerabilities, improves the user experience (understandable errors) and raises the stability of the system. It is recommended to cover such scenarios with automatic tests (unit/integration) and to use validation libraries (Joi, Yup, Zod).","function validateUserInput(input) {\\n  if (input === null || input === undefined) {\\n    return { valid: false, error: 'Input is required' };\\n  }\\n  if (typeof input !== 'string') {\\n    return { valid: false, error: 'Input must be a string' };\\n  }\\n  const trimmed = input.trim();\\n  if (trimmed.length === 0) {\\n    return { valid: false, error: 'Input cannot be empty' };\\n  }\\n  if (trimmed.length > 255) {\\n    return { valid: false, error: 'Input exceeds maximum length' };\\n  }\\n  // The check for SQL injections\\n  const sqlPattern = /('|--|;|\\\\bOR\\\\b|\\\\bAND\\\\b)/i;\\n  if (sqlPattern.test(trimmed)) {\\n    return { valid: false, error: 'Input contains forbidden characters' };\\n  }\\n  return { valid: true, value: trimmed };\\n}"],[732,"An authorisation token is a string the server issues after the user has successfully logged in. The client sends it in every request in order to prove its identity. The server checks the token and, if it is valid, permits access to the resources. Tokens often contain information about the user and about the period of validity. They replace the storage of sessions on the server, making the system scalable.","What is an authorisation token?\\nAn authorisation token is a compact, self-sufficient way of passing data about a user between a client and a server. It is generated by the server after successful authentication (the entry of a login and a password, for example) and is used to confirm the rights of access to protected resources. Unlike sessions, tokens do not require state to be held on the server, which makes scaling simpler.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe process of a token's work can be broken up into several steps:\\n\\n- The user sends their credentials (login and password) to the server.\\n- The server checks them and, if they are correct, creates a token signed with a secret key.\\n- The client saves the token (usually in localStorage or in a cookie) and sends it in the Authorization header of every request.\\n- The server checks the token's signature and, if it is valid, handles the request.\\nAn example in JavaScript (JWT)\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nTokens are widely used in REST APIs, microservice architecture and single-page applications (SPAs). They let you easily implement authorisation without holding sessions on the server, which matters especially during horizontal scaling.\\nConclusion: Authorisation tokens are a modern, secure way of managing access, which is worth applying in distributed systems and APIs where scalability and the absence of state on the server matter.","// The generation of the token on the server (Node.js with jsonwebtoken)\\nconst jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\\nconst token = jwt.sign({ userId: 123, role: 'admin' }, 'secret_key', { expiresIn: '1h' });\\n\\n// Sending the token to the client\\nres.json({ token });\\n\\n// Checking the token on the server\\napp.get('/protected', (req, res) => {\\n  const token = req.headers['authorization']?.split(' ')[1];\\n  try {\\n    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, 'secret_key');\\n    res.json({ message: 'Access permitted', user: decoded });\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    res.status(401).json({ message: 'Invalid token' });\\n  }\\n});"],[733,"Logcat is a utility for viewing the system logs of Android. It lets developers track messages from applications, errors and system events. It is used for debugging through Android Studio or the adb command line.","What is logcat?\\nLogcat is a command-line tool and a built-in function of Android Studio, which gathers and displays system messages, including the logs of applications, errors, warnings and debugging information. It is necessary for diagnosing problems in an application's operation, such as crashes, memory leaks or incorrect behaviour.\\nHow do you use logcat?\\nLogcat can be started through Android Studio in the Logcat panel or through the terminal by means of the \`adb logcat\` command. You can filter the messages by level of importance (VERBOSE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR) and by the tags set in the application's code.\\nAn example of use in code\\n\\nConclusion\\nLogcat is an indispensable tool for debugging Android applications, letting you find and correct errors quickly. It is worth using at every stage of development to monitor an application's behaviour.","import android.util.Log;\\n\\npublic class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {\\n    private static final String TAG = \\"MainActivity\\";\\n\\n    @Override\\n    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {\\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);\\n        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);\\n        \\n        Log.d(TAG, \\"Activity created\\");\\n        try {\\n            // some code\\n        } catch (Exception e) {\\n            Log.e(TAG, \\"Error occurred\\", e);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[734,"To test calculators, the techniques of equivalence partitioning and boundary-value analysis are applied. Divide the input data into equivalence classes (positive, negative, zero numbers) and check the boundaries of each class. It also matters to test scenarios with incorrect data (letters, empty input) and the sequence of operations.","The basics of testing user scenarios with calculations\\nTesting calculators and other systems with computations requires a systematic approach, in order to be sure they work correctly with various input data. The main techniques are equivalence partitioning and boundary-value analysis.\\nEquivalence partitioning\\nDivide all of the possible input data into classes that are handled identically. For a calculator with whole numbers, for example:\\n\\n- Positive numbers (1, 100, 999)\\n- Negative numbers (-1, -50, -999)\\n- Zero (0)\\n- Incorrect data (letters, symbols, an empty string)\\nIt is enough to test one value from each class.\\nBoundary-value analysis\\nErrors often arise at the boundaries of the classes. For a field taking numbers from 0 to 100, check: -1, 0, 1, 99, 100, 101. An example of code for a test in JavaScript:\\n\\nThe user's scenarios\\nCheck the sequences of operations: addition, then multiplication, division by zero. Make sure the priority of the operations is observed. An example of a scenario: the input \\"2 + 3 * 4\\" should give 14 rather than 20.\\nConclusion\\nApply equivalence partitioning and boundary values for every input field, and also test chains of operations. That guarantees the reliability of the calculations in any application.","function testCalculator() {\\n  const calc = new Calculator();\\n  // Boundary values\\n  console.assert(calc.add(-1, 5) === 4, 'Error at -1');\\n  console.assert(calc.add(0, 0) === 0, 'Error at 0');\\n  console.assert(calc.add(100, 1) === 101, 'Error at 100');\\n  // Incorrect input\\n  console.assert(calc.add('a', 5) === NaN, 'Error with a letter');\\n}"],[735,"Before modules appeared in JavaScript, developers used various methods to organise code, such as global variables, function scopes and objects. These methods often led to problems connected with name conflicts and the complexity of managing dependencies, since all of the data and the functions were in a single global scope.","Before modules became the standard in JavaScript, developers used several approaches to organise code:\\n\\n- Global variables: Many functions and variables were declared in the global scope, which could lead to name conflicts and problems with compatibility, especially in large projects. Every new file could overwrite the global variables, which made debugging more difficult.\\n\\n- Function scopes: The use of functions to create local scopes, in order to hide variables from the global scope.\\n\\n- Objects: The creation of objects to group related functions and data. That still did not solve the problem of managing dependencies and name conflicts, however.\\n\\n- An IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression): An immediately invoked function expression creates a local scope, preventing the pollution of the global namespace. It lets you isolate code and manage dependencies.\\n\\nWith the introduction of modules in JavaScript the organisation of code was improved. Modules let you isolate functionality, avoid name conflicts and make managing dependencies simpler, ensuring a cleaner, more structured architecture for applications.","// script1.js \\nvar myVar = 'Hello'; \\n// script2.js \\nconsole.log(myVar); // Hello\\n\\nfunction myFunction() {\\n  var localVar = 'I am local'; \\n} \\nconsole.log(localVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: localVar is not defined\\n\\nvar myModule = {\\n  myVar: 'Hello',    \\n  myFunction: function() {\\n      console.log(this.myVar);    \\n  } \\n};\\n\\n(function() {\\n  var name = 'Module';\\n  function greet() {\\n    console.log('Hello from ' + name);\\n  }\\n  greet(); // Hello from Module\\n})();"],[736,"The receipt of push notifications is affected by the user's permission, the working of the Service Worker, a correct subscription through the Push API, the state of the network and the browser's settings. The server's parameters, such as the VAPID keys and the headers of the notifications, also matter.","The main parameters affecting the receipt of push notifications\\nPush notifications in web applications depend on several key factors, beginning with the user's permission and ending with the server's settings. Let us look at them in more detail.\\nThe user's permission\\nThe browser asks the user for permission to send notifications. If the user has declined the request or blocked notifications in the settings, they will not be delivered. The permission can be checked through \`Notification.permission\`.\\nThe Service Worker\\nPush notifications require a registered Service Worker, which handles the \`push\` and \`notificationclick\` events. Without it the notifications will not be displayed, even if a subscription exists.\\nThe subscription through the Push API\\nTo receive notifications the browser must be subscribed to a push service. The subscription is created by means of \`registration.pushManager.subscribe()\` and includes the endpoint and the encryption keys. If the subscription is out of date or has been deleted, the notifications will not arrive.\\nThe state of the network and of the browser\\nNotifications are delivered only with an active internet connection. The browser can restrict push notifications for background tabs or when the battery is low. Some browsers also require the site to have been open at least once.\\nThe server's parameters\\nThe server must send correct headers, including the VAPID keys for authentication. An example of sending a notification in Node.js:\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor the successful delivery of push notifications it is necessary to take into account the user's permissions, the correct configuration of the Service Worker, a current subscription and the right configuration of the server. This mechanism is useful for engaging users and notifying them of important events in real time.","const webpush = require('web-push');\\nconst vapidKeys = { publicKey: '...', privateKey: '...' };\\nwebpush.setVapidDetails('mailto:example@domain.com', vapidKeys.publicKey, vapidKeys.privateKey);\\nconst pushSubscription = { endpoint: '...', keys: { auth: '...', p256dh: '...' } };\\nwebpush.sendNotification(pushSubscription, JSON.stringify({ title: 'Hello', body: 'World' }));"],[737,"Push notifications work through a service worker and the Web Push API. The server sends a request to a push service (Firebase Cloud Messaging, for example), which delivers the notification to the browser. The browser passes it to the service worker, which shows the notification even when the page is closed. For that you have to subscribe the client to push notifications and obtain a PushSubscription object.","How the mechanism for sending push notifications is arranged\\nPush notifications let a server send messages to a client even when the web page is not open. That is achieved by a combination of service workers and the Web Push API. A service worker is a background script the browser runs separately from the page, while the Web Push API provides the channel of communication between the server and the browser through a push service.\\nThe main components\\n\\n- The client (the browser) — registers the service worker and subscribes to push notifications through PushManager.subscribe().\\n- The push service — an intermediate server (Firebase Cloud Messaging, for example), which takes requests from the application's server and delivers them to the browser.\\n- The application's server — sends push messages, using the encryption keys from the client's subscription.\\nThe process of its work\\n\\n- The client registers the service worker and calls navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js').\\n- The client subscribes to push: registration.pushManager.subscribe({userVisibleOnly: true, applicationServerKey: publicKey}).\\n- The subscription (PushSubscription) is sent to the server and saved.\\n- The server encrypts the message by means of the keys from the subscription and sends it to the push service (the endpoint from the subscription).\\n- The push service delivers the message to the browser, which wakes the service worker.\\n- The service worker handles the push event and shows the notification through self.registration.showNotification().\\nAn example of code on the client\\n\\nAn example of sending from the server (Node.js)\\n\\nConclusion: Push notifications are useful for engaging users — for notifying them of new messages or updates, for example — even when the site is not open. They require HTTPS and support for service workers.","// The registration of the service worker\\nif ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {\\n  navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js')\\n    .then(reg => {\\n      reg.pushManager.subscribe({\\n        userVisibleOnly: true,\\n        applicationServerKey: urlB64ToUint8Array(publicKey)\\n      }).then(sub => {\\n        fetch('/subscribe', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(sub) });\\n      });\\n    });\\n}\\n\\n// The handling in sw.js\\nself.addEventListener('push', event => {\\n  const data = event.data.json();\\n  event.waitUntil(\\n    self.registration.showNotification(data.title, {\\n      body: data.body,\\n      icon: '/icon.png'\\n    })\\n  );\\n});\\n\\nconst webpush = require('web-push');\\nwebpush.setVapidDetails('mailto:example@domain.com', publicKey, privateKey);\\n\\nconst pushSubscription = {\\n  endpoint: '...',\\n  keys: { auth: '...', p256dh: '...' }\\n};\\n\\nwebpush.sendNotification(pushSubscription, JSON.stringify({\\n  title: 'Hello',\\n  body: 'World'\\n}));"],[738,"To check an API response you first have to check the HTTP status code (200 OK, for example). Then validate the body of the response against the schema expected, using JSON Schema or libraries of the Joi kind. It is also worth checking the headers and the response time, and handling errors.","Checking the correctness of an API response\\nWhen working with an API it matters to be sure the response matches expectations. That includes checking the status code, the structure of the data, the types of the fields and the possible errors. Without such a check an application may handle the data incorrectly or crash.\\nThe main steps of the check\\n\\n- The status code: Make sure the response code corresponds to a successful request (2xx) or handles errors (4xx, 5xx).\\n- The headers: Check Content-Type, Cache-Control and other important headers.\\n- The body of the response: Validate the structure of the JSON, the data types and the mandatory fields.\\n- The response time: Make sure the response arrives within the permissible timeout.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nSummary\\nChecking an API response is mandatory for building resilient applications. Use a combination of checking the status code, validating the schema and handling errors, in order to guarantee the correct working of the integrations.","fetch('https://api.example.com/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // The check of the schema\\n    if (typeof data.id !== 'number' || !data.name) {\\n      throw new Error('Invalid response structure');\\n    }\\n    console.log('Valid response:', data);\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Validation failed:', error));"],[739,"To check the mapping of data between the front end and the back end you have to use contract testing — by means of Pact, for example. It is also useful to write unit tests for the serialisers and deserialisers, and integration tests that check the complete cycle of passing data. It matters to make sure the data types, the names of the fields and the structure of the JSON match the API specification.","Checking the correctness of the mapping of data\\nThe mapping of data between the front end and the back end is the process of converting data from the format used on the client into the format the server expects, and vice versa. Errors of mapping can lead to the incorrect working of an application, so it matters to reveal them in good time.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Contract testing — lets you check that the front end and the back end observe a contract agreed in advance (the OpenAPI specification, for example). The tools: Pact, Spring Cloud Contract.\\n- Unit tests — they test the individual functions of serialisation/deserialisation. A check that an object on the front end is correctly converted into JSON and back, for example.\\n- Integration tests — they check the complete cycle: sending a request from the front end, the handling on the back end, the receipt of the response and its correct display.\\nAn example of code (JavaScript, front end)\\n\\nAn example of a contract test (Pact)\\n\\nConclusion: checking the mapping of data is critical for an application's reliability. Use contract testing for automatic verification, and unit tests for the isolated checking of the conversion logic. That is especially useful in microservice architecture and when the API changes frequently.","// The check of the mapping when sending data\\nfunction mapUserToApi(user) {\\n  return {\\n    id: user.id,\\n    fullName: \`\${user.firstName} \${user.lastName}\`,\\n    email: user.email.toLowerCase()\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The test\\ntest('mapUserToApi correctly maps user data', () => {\\n  const user = { id: 1, firstName: 'John', lastName: 'Doe', email: 'John@Example.com' };\\n  const result = mapUserToApi(user);\\n  expect(result).toEqual({ id: 1, fullName: 'John Doe', email: 'john@example.com' });\\n});\\n\\n// The consumer (the front end) defines the expectations\\nconst provider = new Pact({\\n  consumer: 'Frontend',\\n  provider: 'Backend',\\n});\\n\\nawait provider.addInteraction({\\n  state: 'user exists',\\n  uponReceiving: 'a request for user data',\\n  withRequest: { method: 'GET', path: '/users/1' },\\n  willRespondWith: {\\n    status: 200,\\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n    body: { id: 1, fullName: 'John Doe', email: 'john@example.com' }\\n  }\\n});"],[740,"A breakpoint is a special mark in code at which the execution of the program is suspended. That lets a developer analyse the state of the variables and the flow of execution step by step. It is used in the debuggers of IDEs to search for errors and check logic.","What is a breakpoint?\\nA breakpoint is a debugging tool that suspends the execution of a program at a given place. That lets a developer or a tester investigate the application's current state: the values of the variables, the call stack, the memory and so on.\\nHow is it used during testing?\\nDuring testing a breakpoint helps localise defects, check the correctness of the handling of data and analyse the behaviour of code in specific scenarios. You can set a breakpoint before the call of a function and check the input parameters, for example.\\nAn example of use in an IDE (VS Code, WebStorm)\\n\\nWhen it is started with the debugger, the execution will stop at the line with the breakpoint. You can see that \`isPremium\` is equal to \`true\` and that \`discount\` has not yet been assigned.\\nConclusion\\nA breakpoint is an indispensable tool for debugging and testing, letting you analyse the execution of code in detail. It is especially useful with complex algorithms, when working with asynchrony or when searching for elusive bugs.","function calculateDiscount(price, isPremium) {\\n  let discount = 0;\\n  if (isPremium) {\\n    discount = 0.2; // the breakpoint here\\n  }\\n  return price * (1 - discount);\\n}\\n\\n// Running in debugging mode\\nconsole.log(calculateDiscount(100, true));"],[741,"Test coverage is assessed by means of metrics such as the coverage of lines, branches, functions or conditions. Tools of the Istanbul (JavaScript) or Coverage.py (Python) kind analyse which code was executed during the tests. High coverage does not guarantee the absence of bugs, but it helps reveal the parts that have not been checked.","What is test coverage?\\nTest coverage is a metric showing which part of the source code was executed during a run of the tests. It helps developers understand how fully the tests check an application's functionality. It matters to remember, however, that a high percentage of coverage does not mean the absence of errors — the tests may be written incorrectly or may not check the boundary cases.\\nThe main metrics of coverage\\n\\n- Line coverage — the percentage of lines of code that were executed at least once.\\n- Branch coverage — the percentage of branches (if-else, for example) that were passed through.\\n- Function coverage — the percentage of functions called in the tests.\\n- Condition coverage — the percentage of logical conditions that took the values true/false.\\nAn example of the assessment by means of a tool\\nFor JavaScript, Istanbul (or its wrapper nyc) is often used. An example of the configuration:\\n\\nAfter it is started, nyc will print a report: lines, branches, functions — all as percentages. If some line is not executed, it will be highlighted.\\nHow to interpret the results\\n100% coverage is good, but it is not a panacea. If a test calls a function but does not check its returned value, for example, the coverage of lines will be high but the quality low. It is recommended to aim for coverage of 70-80% for the critical modules, but always to supplement it with an analysis of the boundary cases and tests for errors.\\nConclusion: The assessment of test coverage is a useful tool for quality control, but it should be used in combination with other practices, such as code review and boundary-value testing. That helps reduce the number of bugs in production.","// package.json\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"test\\": \\"nyc mocha\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of a test\\nconst sum = (a, b) => a + b;\\nconst result = sum(1, 2);\\nconsole.log(result); // 3"],[742,"A breakpoint suspends the execution of code at the place stated, letting you analyse the state of the program step by step. A rewrite replaces the content of an HTTP response or request on the fly, without changing the source code. A breakpoint is used to debug logic, while a rewrite is used to test various scenarios of the server's responses.","The main difference\\nA breakpoint and a rewrite are two different tools in the browser's DevTools, which solve different tasks. A breakpoint suspends the execution of JavaScript at a given line of code, letting you investigate the variables, the call stack and the state of the application. A rewrite (or Overrides) lets you substitute the content of a network request or response without changing the source code on the server.\\nWhen a breakpoint is used\\nA breakpoint is applied for step-by-step debugging: you set a breakpoint in the code, and the execution stops. You can check the values of the variables, execute code in the console, go through it step by step. That is indispensable when searching for logical errors, incorrect computations or unexpected behaviour of functions.\\n\\nWhen a rewrite is used\\nA rewrite lets you override the server's response for a specific URL. You can replace a JSON response, an HTML file or even an image. That is useful for testing different states of the UI without changing the back end: imitating a server error, an empty list or specific data, for example.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- A breakpoint works at the level of the execution of the code, a rewrite at the level of the network requests.\\n- A breakpoint requires the execution to be stopped, a rewrite happens transparently for the application.\\n- A breakpoint helps debug logic, a rewrite helps test different scenarios of data.\\nConclusion: Use a breakpoint to search for errors in code, and a rewrite to check the behaviour of the interface with various server responses without changing the back end.","// An example: debugging a function with a breakpoint\\nfunction calculateTotal(items) {\\n  let total = 0;\\n  for (let item of items) {\\n    total += item.price * item.quantity; // here we set the breakpoint\\n  }\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\n// An example: overriding an API response\\n// In DevTools -> Network -> right click on the request -> Override content\\n// We substitute the response with:\\n{\\n  \\"users\\": [],\\n  \\"error\\": \\"Service unavailable\\"\\n}"],[743,"WebSocket is a protocol providing a persistent two-way connection between a client and a server. Unlike HTTP, it requires no repeated requests to obtain data. That lets you exchange messages in real time. WebSocket is often used in chats, online games and trading platforms.","What is WebSocket?\\nWebSocket is a communication protocol working over TCP, which provides a full-duplex channel between a client and a server. It begins with an HTTP handshake, after which the connection is \\"upgraded\\" to WebSocket, and both sides can send data at any moment without additional requests.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe client sends an HTTP request with the \`Upgrade: websocket\` header. The server responds with the code 101 Switching Protocols, and the connection becomes persistent. After that the data is passed in the form of frames (messages) rather than HTTP packets.\\nAn example of code (a client in JavaScript)\\n\\nWhere is it used?\\n\\n- Chats and messengers (Slack, WhatsApp Web, for example)\\n- Online games (multiplayer modes)\\n- Exchange and trading platforms (the updating of quotations)\\n- The joint editing of documents (Google Docs)\\nConclusion\\nWebSocket is ideal for applications where low latency and a constant exchange of data are required. It is worth applying instead of HTTP polling in order to save traffic and raise responsiveness.","const socket = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/chat');\\n\\nsocket.onopen = () => {\\n  console.log('The connection has been established');\\n  socket.send('Hello, server!');\\n};\\n\\nsocket.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log('Received:', event.data);\\n};\\n\\nsocket.onclose = () => {\\n  console.log('The connection has been closed');\\n};"],[744,"No, a 400 error (Bad Request) always points to a problem on the client's side — an incorrect syntax of the request or the absence of mandatory parameters, for example. If the problem is on the server's side, 5xx codes should be returned, such as 500 Internal Server Error or 502 Bad Gateway. The use of 400 for server errors breaks the HTTP standard and is misleading.","An understanding of HTTP statuses\\nHTTP statuses divide into classes: 2xx — success, 3xx — redirection, 4xx — a client error, 5xx — a server error. The code 400 (Bad Request) belongs to the 4xx class and signals that the request from the client has been formed incorrectly. If the client has sent JSON with an incorrect syntax or has not stated a mandatory field, for example, the server can respond with 400.\\nWhy 400 is unsuitable for server problems\\nIf the problem is on the server's side — the database is unavailable, the server is overloaded or an internal error has occurred, for example — the correct response will be a 5xx code. The use of 400 in such cases breaks the semantics of the protocol and can lead to incorrect handling on the client: the client may send the same request again, thinking it has corrected the error, although the problem is not with it.\\nExamples\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways use correct HTTP statuses: 4xx for client errors, 5xx for server errors. That ensures the correct handling of errors on the client and makes debugging simpler.","// Incorrect: the server returned 400 on an internal error\\napp.get('/data', (req, res) => {\\n  try {\\n    // ... the logic\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    res.status(400).json({ error: 'Internal error' }); // An error!\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// Correct: the server returned 500 on an internal error\\napp.get('/data', (req, res) => {\\n  try {\\n    // ... the logic\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });\\n  }\\n});"],[745,"It is necessary to clear the cache when the functionality being tested depends on current data and the cache may hold stale values. That is especially important when testing changes in code, configurations or data, in order to avoid false results. Clearing the cache is also required when checking performance after changes, in order to measure the real execution time without the influence of cached data.","Why clear the cache during testing?\\nCaching speeds up the work of applications by saving the results of previous operations. During testing, however, that can lead to false results if the cache contains stale data. Clearing the cache guarantees that the tests are carried out with the current state of the system, which is especially important when checking changes in code, configurations or data.\\nWhen is it necessary to clear the cache?\\n\\n- After a change of code or configurations: If you have changed the application's logic, the cache may hold the old results, and the tests will show incorrect behaviour.\\n- When testing performance: To measure the real execution time without the influence of cached data, it is necessary to clear the cache before running the tests.\\n- When testing integrations: If the system uses external caches (Redis, for example), clearing them helps avoid the influence of data from other tests.\\n- When checking the correctness of data: If functionality that is supposed to update the cache is being tested, clearing it lets you be sure the new data is being saved correctly.\\nAn example in JavaScript using Redis\\n\\nConclusion\\nClearing the cache during testing is necessary to ensure the isolation of the tests and to obtain trustworthy results. That is especially important in CI/CD pipelines, where every test has to be independent. Use the clearing of the cache before every test or set of tests, in order to avoid false positives and errors.","const redis = require('redis');\\nconst client = redis.createClient();\\n\\n// Clearing the cache before the test\\nbeforeEach(async () => {\\n  await client.flushall();\\n});\\n\\n// The test, which checks the obtaining of data from the cache\\ntest('should return cached data', async () => {\\n  await client.set('key', 'value');\\n  const result = await client.get('key');\\n  expect(result).toBe('value');\\n});"],[746,"Circular dependencies arise when two or more modules depend on one another, creating a closed cycle. That can lead to errors and unpredictable behaviour, since one of the modules may not be fully loaded when an attempt is made to use it. To solve this problem you can use refactoring of the code to eliminate the cycles, apply dependency injection or use lazy loading.","Circular dependencies are the situation where two or more modules depend on one another, creating a closed cycle. That can cause problems, especially when one of the modules cannot be initialised because its dependency has not yet been loaded.\\nThe problems of circular dependencies:\\n\\n- Runtime errors: If module A depends on module B and module B depends on module A, that can lead to errors during execution, since one of the modules may be unavailable.\\n\\n- Undefined behaviour: When an attempt is made to gain access to the properties or methods of a circular dependency, undefined behaviour can arise, since one of the modules may be partially loaded.\\n\\nThe methods of solving it:\\n\\n- Refactoring the code: One of the most effective ways of avoiding circular dependencies is to review the structure of your modules and divide the functionality. You can move the common functions or variables out into a separate module, for example.\\n\\n- Dependency injection: Use dependency injection to pass dependencies as parameters, which can help break the cycles.\\n\\n- Lazy loading: Use dynamic imports to load modules only when necessary, which can help avoid problems with circular dependencies.\\n\\nIt thus matters to manage the dependencies in your applications deliberately, in order to avoid circular dependencies and the problems connected with them.","// common.js \\nexport function commonFunction() {\\n  // ... \\n} \\n// moduleA.js \\nimport { commonFunction } from './common.js'; \\n// moduleB.js \\nimport { commonFunction } from './common.js';\\n\\nfunction moduleA(dependency) {\\n  // Use dependency here \\n} \\nfunction moduleB() {\\n  moduleA(moduleB); \\n}\\n\\nasync function loadModuleB() {\\n  const moduleB = await import('./moduleB.js');    \\n  moduleB.someFunction(); \\n}"],[747,"The main data formats in APIs are JSON and XML. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is lightweight, convenient to read and parse, and widely used in REST APIs. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is stricter, with support for schemas and namespaces, and is often applied in SOAP APIs. YAML, Protocol Buffers and Form Data are also met.","The main data formats in APIs\\nWhen developing an API, a key aspect is the choice of the data format for exchanging information between the client and the server. The most popular formats are JSON and XML, each of which has its own peculiarities and areas of application.\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation)\\nJSON is a textual format based on the syntax of JavaScript. It is lightweight, easily read by a human and quickly parsed by a machine. JSON supports simple data types: strings, numbers, boolean values, arrays and objects. Thanks to its simplicity and efficiency, JSON has become the standard for REST APIs.\\n\\nXML (eXtensible Markup Language)\\nXML is a stricter, extensible format, which uses tags to describe data. It supports schemas (XSD) for validating the structure, namespaces to avoid conflicts and attributes for additional information. XML is often applied in SOAP APIs and in systems where a high degree of formalisation is required.\\n\\nOther formats\\n\\n- YAML — used in configuration files (Docker Compose, Kubernetes, for example), more rarely in APIs.\\n- Protocol Buffers (protobuf) — a binary format from Google, providing high performance and compactness; applied in gRPC.\\n- Form Data — used to send data from HTML forms, often with multipart/form-data for files.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of data format depends on the project's requirements: JSON suits most modern REST APIs thanks to its simplicity and speed, XML suits systems with rigid requirements as regards validation and compatibility, and protobuf suits highly loaded microservices. An understanding of these formats helps you design and integrate APIs effectively.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"John\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isActive\\": true,\\n  \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"]\\n}\\n\\n<person>\\n  <name>John</name>\\n  <age>30</age>\\n  <isActive>true</isActive>\\n  <hobbies>\\n    <hobby>reading</hobby>\\n    <hobby>coding</hobby>\\n  </hobbies>\\n</person>"],[748,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight format based on key-value pairs, which is easily read and parsed. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is a more heavyweight markup language with tags and attributes, supporting schemas and namespaces. JSON is usually simpler and faster for web APIs, whereas XML is more often used in complex documents and in systems with rigid schemas.","The main differences between JSON and XML\\nJSON and XML are two popular formats for holding and transferring structured data. JSON came from JavaScript and became the standard for web APIs thanks to its simplicity. XML, by contrast, is an older, more powerful markup language, which is widely applied in configuration files, documents and systems where strict validation matters.\\nSyntax and readability\\nJSON uses a minimalist syntax: objects in curly brackets, arrays in square ones, the data represented by key-value pairs. XML uses opening and closing tags, which makes it more cumbersome. An example:\\n\\nData types and support\\nJSON supports a limited set of types: strings, numbers, boolean values, arrays, objects and null. XML has no built-in types — all of the data is strings, which requires additional handling. JSON is more easily parsed in programming languages, especially in JavaScript, where it can be converted into an object by means of \`JSON.parse()\`.\\nApplication\\nJSON dominates in RESTful APIs, configurations (package.json, for example) and web applications. XML is often used in SOAP services, SVG graphics, RSS feeds and systems where a strict schema (XSD) is required.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice between JSON and XML depends on the task: for simple, fast exchanges of data on the web JSON suits better, while for complex documents with validation and namespaces, XML.","// JSON\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"city\\": \\"New York\\"\\n}\\n\\n// XML\\n<person>\\n  <name>Alice</name>\\n  <age>30</age>\\n  <city>New York</city>\\n</person>"],[749,"HTTP is a request-response protocol without the preservation of state, where the client initiates the connection, the server responds and closes it. WebSocket is a full-duplex communication protocol, which, once established through an HTTP handshake, holds a persistent connection, letting the server send data to the client without a request. WebSocket suits chats, games and exchange data, while HTTP suits ordinary web pages and APIs.","The main differences between WebSocket and HTTP\\nHTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the classic application-level protocol, working on the «request-response» model. Every request from the client requires a separate TCP connection (or uses keep-alive, but it still follows the scheme: the client initiates, the server responds). WebSocket is a protocol that, after the initial HTTP handshake (with an upgrade of the headers), switches to a persistent bidirectional connection, letting the server send data at any moment.\\nHow it works in practice\\nIn HTTP the client always initiates the exchange: the browser sends a GET request, the server returns the HTML. To obtain updates (new messages, for example) you have to use polling (periodic requests) or long polling. WebSocket, for its part, holds the connection open once it has been established, and the server can «push» data without waiting for a request.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhen to use what\\n\\n- HTTP — for REST APIs, loading static files, CRUD operations, where a constant flow of data is not needed.\\n- WebSocket — for chats, online games, exchange quotations, joint editing, any real-time applications.\\nConclusion: WebSocket is effective for scenarios with frequent updates from the server, while HTTP remains the standard for ordinary requests. The choice depends on the need for bidirectional communication in real time.","// The client (the browser), WebSocket\\nconst socket = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/chat');\\nsocket.onopen = () => socket.send('Hello!');\\nsocket.onmessage = (event) => console.log('Received:', event.data);\\n\\n// The server (Node.js with the ws library)\\nconst WebSocket = require('ws');\\nconst wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });\\nwss.on('connection', (ws) => {\\n  ws.on('message', (msg) => console.log('Got:', msg));\\n  ws.send('Welcome!');\\n});"],[750,"The body of a request is usually passed in the JSON format, which is lightweight and convenient to read. The form-data format for sending files and URL-encoded for simple forms are also used. The choice of format depends on the type of the data and on the API's requirements.","The formats for passing the body of a request\\nThe body of an HTTP request is used to send data to the server — when creating or updating resources, for example. The most widespread format is JSON (JavaScript Object Notation), which represents data in the form of key-value pairs. JSON is easily read and handled both by a human and by a machine, which makes it the standard for REST APIs.\\nOther popular formats\\n\\n- application/x-www-form-urlencoded — used to send the data of HTML forms, where the data is encoded as key=value, separated by &.\\n- multipart/form-data — applied to upload files, since it lets you pass binary data and text in one request.\\n- text/plain — rarely used, but it can be useful for sending simple text.\\nExamples of code\\nAn example of sending JSON through fetch:\\n\\nAn example of sending form-data:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of the format of the request body depends on the task: JSON suits structured data, form-data suits files, and URL-encoded suits simple forms. An understanding of these formats is necessary for working correctly with APIs and web applications.","fetch('/api/users', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John', age: 30 })\\n});\\n\\nconst formData = new FormData();\\nformData.append('file', fileInput.files[0]);\\nfetch('/upload', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  body: formData\\n});"],[751,"4xx errors arise when a request from the client contains incorrect data or cannot be handled by the server through the client's fault. 404 means the resource has not been found, for example, while 401 means authorisation is required. These errors help a developer understand exactly what went wrong in the request.","What are 4xx errors?\\n4xx errors are a class of HTTP status codes that point to problems on the client's side. The server receives the request but cannot carry it out because of incorrect data, the absence of access rights or an incorrect URL. These errors are critical for debugging and for designing APIs.\\nThe main causes of their arising\\n\\n- 400 Bad Request — an incorrect syntax of the request, incorrect JSON in the body of a POST request, for example.\\n- 401 Unauthorized — the authorisation token is absent or invalid.\\n- 403 Forbidden — the client has no rights to access the resource, even with authorisation.\\n- 404 Not Found — the resource requested does not exist on the server.\\n- 405 Method Not Allowed — an unsupported HTTP method is used (DELETE on a read-only endpoint, for example).\\n- 429 Too Many Requests — the limit of requests has been exceeded (rate limiting).\\nAn example of code for handling a 4xx error on the client (JavaScript)\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n4xx errors are used in REST APIs, web servers (Nginx, Apache) and frameworks (Express, Django). They help the client understand how to correct the request.\\nConclusion: An understanding of 4xx errors is necessary for handling requests correctly and improving the user experience, especially when developing APIs and client-server applications.","fetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      if (response.status === 404) {\\n        throw new Error('The resource has not been found');\\n      } else if (response.status === 401) {\\n        throw new Error('Authorisation is required');\\n      }\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error.message));"],[752,"Besides REST there are GraphQL, gRPC, SOAP, WebSocket and others. GraphQL lets the client request only the data it needs. gRPC uses Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2 for high performance. SOAP is a strict protocol based on XML. WebSocket provides two-way communication in real time.","The alternatives to REST\\nREST is a popular but not the only way of building an API. Depending on the requirements as regards performance, flexibility or the type of data, developers choose other protocols.\\nGraphQL\\nGraphQL is a query language developed by Facebook. It lets the client state precisely which fields it needs, avoiding over-fetching and under-fetching. It is used in complex systems with a multitude of related data.\\n\\ngRPC\\ngRPC is a framework from Google based on HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers. It provides high speed and strict typing. It suits microservices and internal systems.\\n\\nSOAP\\nSOAP is a strict protocol based on XML with support for WS-Security. It is used in corporate systems where reliability and standardisation matter.\\nWebSocket\\nWebSocket is a protocol for a persistent two-way connection. It is applied in chats, games and real-time applications.\\nConclusion: The choice of API depends on the task: GraphQL for flexible queries, gRPC for performance, SOAP for security, WebSocket for real time.","query {\\n  user(id: 1) {\\n    name\\n    email\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nservice UserService {\\n  rpc GetUser (UserRequest) returns (UserResponse);\\n}"],[753,"Logs help you reconstruct the sequence of events that led to a bug. A developer analyses the logging levels (error, warn, info, debug) and the timestamps. In the logs they look for exceptions, unexpected values of variables and anomalies in the system's behaviour. That lets you localise the problem without reproducing it on a local machine.","The role of logs in the analysis of bugs\\nLogging is the recording of the events happening in an application, with the time, the level of importance and the context stated. When analysing bugs, logs become the main source of information, especially if the error is hard to reproduce or happens in production.\\nHow to use logs\\n\\n- Begin by searching for errors of the \`ERROR\` or \`FATAL\` level — they point to critical failures.\\n- Check the timestamps, in order to reconstruct the chronology of the events up to the moment of the error.\\n- Look for related logs by the request ID or the user's session.\\n- Analyse the logs of the \`WARN\` and \`INFO\` levels — they can show preceding anomalies.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example the log of the ERROR level contains the traceback, which helps you find the place of the error quickly.\\nConclusion\\nLogs are an indispensable tool for diagnosing bugs in production. The correct configuration of the logging levels and structured output let you shorten the time it takes to find a problem and raise an application's stability.","import logging\\n\\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\\n\\ndef process_order(order_id):\\n    logger.info(f\\"Processing order {order_id}\\")\\n    try:\\n        # the business logic\\n        result = 10 / 0  # an imitation of an error\\n    except ZeroDivisionError as e:\\n        logger.error(f\\"Failed to process order {order_id}: {e}\\", exc_info=True)\\n        raise"],[754,"The repeated sending of a POST request with the same data can lead to the creation of duplicate resources, if the server does not implement protection against duplication. Unlike GET or PUT, POST is not idempotent — every request can change the state of the server. To prevent duplication, unique identifiers of requests (an idempotency key) or checks on the server's side are often used.","The repeated sending of a POST request\\nThe HTTP method POST is not idempotent — that is, the repeated sending of one and the same request can lead to different results. Unlike GET (safe) or PUT (idempotent), every POST request usually creates a new resource or changes the state of the server. When placing an order, for example, the repeated sending of the form can create several identical orders.\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a simple server in Node.js without protection against duplication:\\n\\nIf the client sends the request twice, two identical orders will appear in the database.\\nThe solutions\\n\\n- An Idempotency Key: the client passes a unique key (a UUID, for example) in the \`Idempotency-Key\` header. The server checks whether such a key has already been handled and returns the previous result.\\n- A check on the server's side: a check of the uniqueness of the email on registration, for example, or the use of transactions in the database.\\n- Optimistic locking: the version of the resource or a timestamp to prevent duplication.\\nAn example with an idempotency key\\n\\nConclusion: the repeated sending of a POST request can cause the duplication of data, so in critical operations (payments, orders) it is mandatory to use mechanisms of idempotency, such as an idempotency key or checks of uniqueness.","app.post('/order', (req, res) => {\\n  const order = { id: Date.now(), ...req.body };\\n  orders.push(order);\\n  res.status(201).json(order);\\n});\\n\\napp.post('/order', (req, res) => {\\n  const key = req.headers['idempotency-key'];\\n  if (cache[key]) return res.json(cache[key]);\\n  const order = { id: key, ...req.body };\\n  orders.push(order);\\n  cache[key] = order;\\n  res.status(201).json(order);\\n});"],[755,"In a POST request the data is passed in the body of the request (the request body). The body can contain various formats, such as JSON, XML or form data (form-data). That lets you send large volumes of data, unlike a GET request, where the data is passed in the URL.","Where is the data passed in a POST request?\\nIn an HTTP POST request the data is passed in the body of the request (the request body). That is the main difference from a GET request, where the data is passed in the URL string. The body of a POST request can contain various formats of data, such as JSON, XML or form data (form-data).\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen you send a POST request, the client (the browser or an application, for example) forms an HTTP message, which includes headers and a body. The headers contain metadata, such as the type of the content (Content-Type), which tells the server how to interpret the body of the request.\\nExamples of code\\nAn example of sending a POST request with JSON data in JavaScript:\\n\\nAn example of sending a POST request with form data in Python using the requests library:\\n\\nConclusion\\nPOST requests are used to send data to a server, especially when the data is sensitive or of a large volume. The body of the request lets you pass structured data in various formats, which makes POST a universal method for creating and updating resources.","fetch('https://api.example.com/data', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: {\\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json'\\n  },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({\\n    name: 'John',\\n    age: 30\\n  })\\n})\\n.then(response => response.json())\\n.then(data => console.log(data));\\n\\nimport requests\\n\\nurl = 'https://api.example.com/login'\\ndata = {'username': 'user', 'password': 'pass'}\\nresponse = requests.post(url, data=data)\\nprint(response.text)"],[756,"Bug-tracking systems are used to register, track and manage errors and tasks. The popular systems: Jira, Redmine, YouTrack, Bugzilla, Trello. They help teams organise the work, assign the people responsible and keep the status of tasks under control.","What are bug-tracking systems?\\nBug-tracking systems (or issue trackers) are tools intended for registering, tracking and managing errors, tasks and requests for improvement in the process of developing software. They let teams hold information about problems centrally, assign the people responsible, establish priorities and keep the execution under control.\\nThe popular systems\\n\\n- Jira — one of the most widespread systems, developed by Atlassian. It supports flexible workflows and integration with Git, CI/CD and other tools.\\n- Redmine — a free system with open source code. It supports several projects, Gantt charts and configurable roles.\\n- YouTrack — from JetBrains, fast and convenient, with support for Agile boards and automation.\\n- Bugzilla — one of the oldest systems, used in open-source projects.\\n- Trello — a simpler tool based on kanban boards; it suits small teams.\\nAn example of use\\nIn Jira you can create a task with a description of the error, state the priority, assign a developer and track the status from \\"Open\\" to \\"Closed\\". An example of the configuration of a workflow:\\n\\nIntegration with Git lets you link commits with tasks automatically — through a key in the commit message, for example: \`PROJECT-123 fix login bug\`.\\nConclusion\\nBug-tracking systems are necessary for organising a team's work, raising the transparency of the processes and controlling quality. The choice of system depends on the size of the team, the budget and the requirements as regards functionality.","The statuses: Open -> In Progress -> Resolved -> Closed\\nThe transitions: Open -> In Progress (assign), In Progress -> Resolved (fix), Resolved -> Closed (verify)"],[757,"The Document Object Model (DOM) is the representation of an HTML document in the form of a tree of objects. Every part of the document (tags, attributes, text) is represented as a node in that tree, which lets developers change the content and the structure of the page programmatically by means of JavaScript. Thanks to the DOM you can add, delete or change elements on a web page dynamically.","The Document Object Model (DOM) is the interface browsers use to represent and manipulate the structure of an HTML document. The DOM organises the elements of a web page in the form of a tree of nodes, where every node represents a part of the document, such as tags, text or attributes.\\nThe structure of the DOM:\\nWhen an HTML document is loaded, the browser creates an object representation in which every element (\`<div>\`, \`<p>\`, \`<ul>\`, for example) becomes a node of the tree. These nodes are linked with one another and can contain child nodes. The structure of a document can look like this, for example:\\n\\nThe manipulation of the DOM by means of JavaScript:\\nDevelopers can interact with the DOM through JavaScript, which lets them change the content of a page dynamically. By means of the DOM's methods you can add new elements, change attributes or delete existing nodes. Here is an example:\\n\\nInteraction with the DOM:\\nThe DOM provides developers with a multitude of methods for working with elements, such as \`getElementById\`, \`getElementsByClassName\`, \`querySelector\` and others. These methods let you find and change the elements on a page. The DOM also reacts to events such as clicks or key presses, which lets you build interactive web applications.\\nThe DOM is thus a powerful tool, which makes the creation of dynamic, interactive web pages possible by providing access to the structure and the content of a document.","Document\\n└── html\\n    ├── head\\n    └── body\\n        └── div\\n            └── p\\n                └── ul\\n                    ├── li\\n                    └── li\\n\\n// We create a new element \\nconst newElement = document.createElement('p'); \\nnewElement.textContent = 'Hello, World!'; // We add the element to the body of the document \\ndocument.body.appendChild(newElement);"],[758,"The interception of HTTP requests lets you change or analyse a request before it is sent to the server. In JavaScript that is often done through interceptors in Axios or through middleware in Express. You can also use Service Workers for interception at the level of the browser. That is useful for adding tokens, for logging or for handling errors.","What is the interception of HTTP requests?\\nThe interception of HTTP requests is a technique that lets you intercept and modify requests before they are sent to the server, or after the response is received. It is widely used to add authentication headers, for logging, for handling errors or for caching.\\nAn example with Axios (the client side)\\nAxios provides built-in interceptors for requests and responses. Here is how you can add an authorisation token to every request:\\n\\nAn example with Express (the server side)\\nOn the server you can use middleware to intercept and modify requests:\\n\\nService Workers (interception in the browser)\\nService Workers let you intercept every network request from the browser, even when the application is offline:\\n\\nConclusion: the interception of HTTP requests is a powerful tool for the centralised management of network interactions. It is worth applying for authentication, logging, the handling of errors and caching, in order to make the code cleaner and avoid duplication.","import axios from 'axios';\\n\\naxios.interceptors.request.use(\\n  config => {\\n    config.headers.Authorization = \`Bearer \${localStorage.getItem('token')}\`;\\n    return config;\\n  },\\n  error => Promise.reject(error)\\n);\\n\\naxios.interceptors.response.use(\\n  response => response,\\n  error => {\\n    if (error.response.status === 401) {\\n      // redirection to the login\\n    }\\n    return Promise.reject(error);\\n  }\\n);\\n\\nconst express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\\n  console.log(\`\${req.method} \${req.url}\`);\\n  req.customData = 'added by the middleware';\\n  next();\\n});\\n\\napp.get('/data', (req, res) => {\\n  res.json({ message: req.customData });\\n});\\n\\nself.addEventListener('fetch', event => {\\n  event.respondWith(\\n    caches.match(event.request).then(cached => {\\n      return cached || fetch(event.request);\\n    })\\n  );\\n});"],[759,"To determine where the error is, first check whether a response is arriving from the server. Open the Network tab in the browser's developer tools. If the request goes out but there is no response or the status is 5xx, the problem is on the back end. If the response arrives but the interface works incorrectly, the error is on the front end. You can also check the browser's console for JavaScript errors.","To localise an error between the front end and the back end it is necessary to analyse the network interaction and the behaviour of the application. The main tool is the Network tab in the browser's DevTools. It shows every HTTP request, its status, the headers and the bodies of the responses.\\nThe steps of the diagnosis\\n\\n- Open DevTools (F12) and go to the Network tab.\\n- Reproduce the action that raises the error.\\n- Find the corresponding request (usually XHR or Fetch).\\n- Check the status of the response: 2xx — success, 4xx — a client error (404 — not found, 400 — a bad request, for example), 5xx — a server error.\\n- If the status is 5xx or the request did not get through, the problem is on the back end.\\n- If the status is 2xx but the interface displays incorrect data, the error is on the front end.\\nAn example of code for the check\\n\\nAdditional methods\\n\\n- Check the browser's console for JavaScript errors (the red messages).\\n- Use the back end's tools: the server's logs, error monitoring (Sentry, Datadog).\\n- Check whether the API works through Postman or curl — that isolates the front end.\\nConclusion: a systematic approach with a check of the network requests and of the statuses of the responses lets you determine quickly which side is responsible for an error and speed up its correction.","fetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      // A status of 4xx or 5xx — an error on the back end\\n      console.error('Backend error:', response.status);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // The data has been received — we check the correctness of the display\\n    renderData(data);\\n  })\\n  .catch(err => {\\n    // A network error — a problem with the connection or the server\\n    console.error('Network error:', err);\\n  });"],[760,"The main HTTP methods: GET (obtaining data), POST (creating a resource), PUT (updating a resource), DELETE (deleting a resource). There are also PATCH (a partial update), HEAD (obtaining the headers without a body), OPTIONS (checking the available methods). Every method has its own meaning and is used in REST APIs for different operations.","The main HTTP methods\\nThe HTTP protocol defines several methods (verbs), which point to the action desired with a resource on the server. The most frequently used methods are GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, HEAD and OPTIONS. Each of them has a strictly defined purpose and semantics.\\nGET\\nUsed to obtain data from the server. GET requests should not change the state of the server (they are idempotent). The parameters are passed in the URL string of the request.\\n\\nPOST\\nIntended for the creation of a new resource on the server. The data is passed in the body of the request. It is not idempotent — a repeated POST can create several identical resources.\\n\\nPUT\\nUsed for the complete update of an existing resource. It is idempotent — a repeated request with the same data will not change the state. If the resource does not exist, it can be created.\\n\\nDELETE\\nDeletes the resource stated. It is idempotent — a repeated DELETE will return the same result (usually 404).\\n\\nPATCH\\nApplied for the partial update of a resource. Unlike PUT, it replaces only the fields passed, without touching the rest.\\n\\nHEAD and OPTIONS\\nHEAD is the same as GET, but the server returns only the headers without the body of the response. OPTIONS lets you find out which methods the server supports for a specific resource.\\nConclusion: An understanding of the HTTP methods is necessary for designing RESTful APIs, writing client-server applications and debugging network requests. The correct choice of method makes an API intuitively understandable and conformant to the standards.","GET /api/users?page=1 HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\n\\nPOST /api/users HTTP/1.1\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"email\\": \\"john@example.com\\"}\\n\\nPUT /api/users/123 HTTP/1.1\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\"name\\": \\"John Updated\\", \\"email\\": \\"john.new@example.com\\"}\\n\\nDELETE /api/users/123 HTTP/1.1\\n\\nPATCH /api/users/123 HTTP/1.1\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\"email\\": \\"new.email@example.com\\"}"],[761,"You can emulate server errors by means of intercepting requests in the browser's developer tools, using mock servers (JSON Server, Mock Service Worker, for example) or changing the API's responses through a proxy (Charles Proxy, for example). You can also change the back end's code temporarily so that it returns the statuses you need. That helps you check how the application handles 4xx errors (404, 403, for example) and shows the user understandable messages.","What is the emulation of server errors?\\nThe emulation of server errors is the process of imitating responses with 4xx codes (400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, for example) in order to test the behaviour of a client application. It is necessary in order to be sure the interface handles errors correctly, shows the user understandable messages and does not break.\\nThe ways of emulating\\n\\n- The browser's developer tools: The Network tab lets you block requests or change responses. In Chrome, for example, you can right-click on a request and choose 'Block request URL'.\\n- Mock servers: Use libraries of the Mock Service Worker (MSW) kind to intercept requests at the level of the Service Worker. An example:\\n\\n- Proxy servers: Tools of the Charles Proxy or Fiddler kind let you intercept traffic and change responses on the fly.\\n- Changing the back end: Temporarily add a condition in the server's code to return the status you need. In Express, for example:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe emulation of errors is used in unit testing, integration testing and the manual testing of a UI. It helps you check scenarios that are difficult to reproduce in real conditions (the temporary unavailability of the server, for example).\\nConclusion: The emulation of server errors is an important practice for ensuring an application's fault tolerance. It lets you reveal problems with the handling of errors in advance and improve the user experience without waiting for real failures.","import { rest } from 'msw'\\nimport { setupServer } from 'msw/node'\\n\\nconst server = setupServer(\\n  rest.get('/api/user', (req, res, ctx) => {\\n    return res(ctx.status(403), ctx.json({ message: 'Forbidden' }))\\n  })\\n)\\n\\nbeforeAll(() => server.listen())\\nafterAll(() => server.close())\\n\\napp.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {\\n  if (req.query.test === 'error') {\\n    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Bad request' })\\n  }\\n  res.json({ data: 'success' })\\n})"],[762,"The parameters in a GET request are passed in the query string (the URL) after the question mark (?). They have the format key=value and are separated by the & symbol. For example: /api/users?page=2&limit=10. That is the standard way of passing data in GET requests, which is used for filtering, sorting and pagination.","How the parameters are passed in a GET request\\nIn an HTTP GET request the parameters are passed in the URL (Uniform Resource Locator) string after the question mark (?). This part of the URL is called the query string. The parameters have the format key=value, and several parameters are separated by the & symbol.\\nAn example of the structure of a URL with parameters\\nLet us look at the URL: \`https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10&sort=name\`. Here:\\n\\n- \`https://api.example.com/users\` — the base path to the resource\\n- \`?\` — the separator indicating the beginning of the parameters\\n- \`page=2\` — the first parameter (the number of the page)\\n- \`&\` — the separator between the parameters\\n- \`limit=10\` — the second parameter (the number of elements on the page)\\n- \`sort=name\` — the third parameter (sorting by name)\\nA practical example in JavaScript\\n\\nThe peculiarities of passing parameters in GET\\n\\n- Visibility: The parameters are visible in the URL, so do not use GET to pass confidential data (passwords, tokens).\\n- The restriction on length: A URL has a restriction on its length (usually 2048 characters), so GET is unsuitable for passing large volumes of data.\\n- Caching: GET requests with parameters can be cached by the browser and by proxy servers, which speeds up repeated requests.\\n- Idempotency: GET requests should not change the state of the server (only the reading of data).\\nConclusion\\nThe parameters in a GET request are passed through the query string in the URL. This method is convenient for filtering, sorting and paginating data, but it is unsuitable for passing sensitive information or large volumes of data. Use GET with parameters for safe, idempotent requests for the reading of data.","// The forming of a GET request with parameters\\nconst baseUrl = 'https://api.example.com/users';\\nconst params = {\\n  page: 2,\\n  limit: 10,\\n  sort: 'name'\\n};\\n\\n// The conversion of the object into a query string\\nconst queryString = Object.entries(params)\\n  .map(([key, value]) => \`\${encodeURIComponent(key)}=\${encodeURIComponent(value)}\`)\\n  .join('&');\\n\\nconst fullUrl = \`\${baseUrl}?\${queryString}\`;\\nconsole.log(fullUrl);\\n// https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10&sort=name\\n\\n// Carrying out the request\\nfetch(fullUrl)\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data));"],[763,"To check logical conditions, use unit tests covering every possible combination of input data. Pay special attention to the boundary cases, such as null, empty values and extreme ranges. Apply the technique of equivalence partitioning and boundary-value analysis. That helps reveal errors in if and switch conditions and in ternary operators.","Checking logical conditions\\nLogical conditions are the foundation of taking decisions in code. Errors in them lead to the unpredictable behaviour of an application. To check them, a combination of unit testing and manual analysis is applied.\\nThe methods of checking\\n\\n- The coverage of every branch: every if/else has to be tested.\\n- Boundary values: test the extreme points of the ranges (0, the maximum number, an empty string).\\n- Combinatorial testing: check every possible combination of the boolean variables.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nIn this example three key scenarios have been checked: an administrator, an adult user and a minor. If we had missed the case with isAdmin=true and age=0, the test would not have revealed the potential error.\\nConclusion\\nThe systematic checking of logical conditions through tests with the coverage of the boundary cases and of every branch substantially reduces the likelihood of bugs in production. That is especially important in critical modules, such as authorisation, the validation of data and business logic.","function checkAccess(age, isAdmin) {\\n  if (isAdmin) return true;\\n  if (age >= 18) return true;\\n  return false;\\n}\\n\\n// The tests\\ntest('admin always has access', () => {\\n  expect(checkAccess(15, true)).toBe(true);\\n});\\ntest('adult non-admin has access', () => {\\n  expect(checkAccess(20, false)).toBe(true);\\n});\\ntest('minor non-admin denied', () => {\\n  expect(checkAccess(17, false)).toBe(false);\\n});"],[764,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a textual format for holding and transferring data. It consists of \\"key-value\\" pairs, where the keys are strings in double quotation marks and the values can be strings, numbers, objects, arrays, boolean values or null. The data is organised into objects (in curly brackets) and arrays (in square brackets).","The basics of the structure of JSON\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-exchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript. It is used to transfer structured data between a server and a client, and also to hold configurations. The structure of JSON consists of two main constructions: the object and the array.\\nThe object\\nAn object is an unordered set of \\"key-value\\" pairs, enclosed in curly brackets {}. The keys are always strings in double quotation marks, while the values can be of any permissible type. An example:\\n\\nThe array\\nAn array is an ordered list of values, enclosed in square brackets []. The values are separated by commas. An example:\\n\\nThe permissible types of value\\n\\n- A string (in double quotation marks)\\n- A number (whole or floating-point)\\n- A boolean value (true or false)\\n- null\\n- An object\\n- An array\\nAn example of a complex structure\\n\\nIn this example the user object contains a string, a number, an array and null. The nesting can be of any depth.\\nConclusion\\nJSON is a simple, readable format, which is easily parsed by most programming languages. It is applied for APIs, configuration files and the holding of data, when universality and a minimal size are required.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isStudent\\": false\\n}\\n\\n[\\n  \\"apple\\",\\n  \\"banana\\",\\n  \\"cherry\\"\\n]\\n\\n{\\n  \\"user\\": {\\n    \\"id\\": 1,\\n    \\"name\\": \\"Bob\\",\\n    \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"],\\n    \\"address\\": null\\n  },\\n  \\"active\\": true\\n}"],[765,"The main mistakes when testing an API include ignoring boundary values, testing only the positive scenarios, the absence of a check of the status codes and the headers, and the neglect of security. Performance and the handling of errors are also often forgotten. It matters to check not only the responses but also the correctness of the requests.","The main mistakes when testing an API\\nThe testing of an API is a critically important stage of development, which is often carried out with mistakes. The most widespread problems are connected with insufficient coverage of the scenarios and with ignoring the specifics of the protocols.\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- Testing only the positive scenarios: checking only successful requests without taking into account errors of input, of authorisation or incorrect data.\\n- Ignoring boundary values: the extreme values of the parameters are not checked — empty strings, zero numbers or very large volumes of data, for example.\\n- The absence of a check of the status codes and the headers: a focus only on the body of the response, without an analysis of the HTTP statuses (200, 400, 500) and the headers (Content-Type, Cache-Control).\\n- The neglect of security: vulnerabilities such as SQL injections, XSS or insufficient authentication are not tested.\\n- Insufficient testing of performance: the API's behaviour under load is not checked, which can lead to failures in production.\\n- Ignoring the handling of errors: it is not checked how the API reacts to incorrect requests, timeouts or failures of the server.\\nAn example of code for testing\\n\\nConclusion\\nAvoid these mistakes, in order to ensure the reliability and the security of an API. Always test the negative scenarios, the boundary values and performance, and also use automated tests for a regular check.","// An example in JavaScript using fetch and Jest\\ntest('the API returns 400 with incorrect data', async () => {\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/users', {\\n    method: 'POST',\\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n    body: JSON.stringify({ name: '' })\\n  });\\n  expect(response.status).toBe(400);\\n  const data = await response.json();\\n  expect(data.error).toBe('Name is required');\\n});"],[766,"In the body of a response the data the client requested, or information about an error, is usually passed. That can be JSON, XML, HTML or another format. It matters to check that the data matches the structure and the types expected, and also that it is correctly serialised. When requesting a list of users, for example, the body of the response should contain an array of objects with the fields id, name and email.","What is the body of a response?\\nThe response body is the part of an HTTP response that contains the actual data passed from the server to the client. It follows the headers and can be in various formats, such as JSON, XML, HTML, plain text or binary data. The correct forming of the body of a response is critically important for the correct working of APIs and web applications.\\nWhich data should be in the body of a response?\\nIn the body of a response the following are usually passed:\\n\\n- The data requested (a list of users, information about a product, for example).\\n- Messages about errors with a description of the problem.\\n- Confirmations of the successful carrying out of an operation (when creating a resource, for example).\\nIt matters that the data be structured and match the format expected. For REST APIs, for example, JSON is often used, where the keys and the values have to be correctly escaped.\\nAn example of checking the data in the body of a response\\nLet us look at an example in JavaScript using fetch:\\n\\nIn this example we check that the body of the response is an array of objects with the fields id (a number) and name (a string). That helps avoid errors during the further handling of the data.\\nConclusion\\nChecking the data in the body of a response is necessary to ensure the reliability and the security of an application. It lets you be sure the server returns the structure expected and prevents failures on the client's side. That is especially important when working with external APIs or when developing microservices.","fetch('/api/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // We check that data is an array\\n    if (!Array.isArray(data)) {\\n      throw new Error('An array was expected');\\n    }\\n    // We check the structure of every object\\n    data.forEach(user => {\\n      if (typeof user.id !== 'number' || typeof user.name !== 'string') {\\n        throw new Error('An incorrect structure of the user');\\n      }\\n    });\\n    console.log('The data is correct:', data);\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[767,"The time in a system can be taken from the client (the user's browser) or from the server. The client's time is unreliable because of the time zone and the possibility of the user changing it. The server's time is usually precise, synchronised through NTP, and is used for critical operations — logging or transactions, for example.","The sources of time in web applications\\nThe time in a system can come from two main sources: the client side (the user's browser) and the server side. The choice of source depends on the requirements as regards precision, security and the context of use.\\nThe client's time\\nThe client's time is obtained through JavaScript by means of the \`Date\` object or the \`performance.now()\` API. It is based on the system clock of the user's device. The main shortcomings:\\n\\n- The user can change the time by hand.\\n- The time zone may differ from UTC.\\n- There is no guarantee of synchronisation with the real time.\\nAn example of obtaining the client's time:\\n\\nThe client's time suits the display of the user's local time, but not critical operations.\\nThe server's time\\nThe server's time is usually precise, since servers are synchronised through the NTP (Network Time Protocol). It is used for logging, transactions, the generation of tokens and other operations where trustworthiness matters. An example of obtaining the time on the server (Node.js):\\n\\nThe server can pass the time to the client through HTTP headers (the \`Date\` one, for example) or through an API.\\nConclusion\\nFor tasks requiring precision and security, always use the server's time. Apply the client's time only for the display in the UI or for non-critical computations where an error is permissible.","const clientTime = new Date();\\nconsole.log('Client time:', clientTime.toISOString());\\n\\nconst serverTime = new Date();\\nconsole.log('Server time:', serverTime.toISOString());"],[768,"A live DOM collection is a list of elements that updates automatically when the document changes. If you use methods such as \`getElementsByTagName\`, for example, any changes in the corresponding elements on the page will automatically be reflected in that collection. That distinguishes live collections from static ones, which stay unchanged after they have been created.","A live DOM collection is a type of collection that updates automatically when the structure of the DOM changes. That means that if you change the HTML document (add or delete elements), the live collection will reflect those changes without the need to request the elements again.\\nExamples of live collections:\\n\\n- \`document.getElementsByTagName()\`: Returns a live collection of every element with the tag given.\\n\\n- \`document.getElementsByClassName()\`: Returns a live collection of every element with the class given.\\n\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nStatic collections:\\nUnlike live collections, static collections do not update automatically. \`document.querySelectorAll()\`, for example, returns a static collection, which contains the elements at the moment the method was called, and any changes in the DOM will not affect that collection.\\n\\nA live DOM collection can thus be convenient when working with dynamic changes of a page, but developers have to be careful not to meet unexpected results when manipulating the elements.","const listItems = document.getElementsByTagName('li'); \\nconsole.log(listItems.length); // Suppose it is 3 \\n// We add a new list item \\nconst newItem = document.createElement('li'); \\nnewItem.textContent = 'New Item'; \\ndocument.querySelector('ul').appendChild(newItem); \\nconsole.log(listItems.length); // Now it will be 4\\n\\nconst staticItems = document.querySelectorAll('li'); \\nconsole.log(staticItems.length); // Suppose it is 3 \\n// We add a new list item \\nconst newItem = document.createElement('li'); \\nnewItem.textContent = 'New Item'; \\ndocument.querySelector('ul').appendChild(newItem); \\nconsole.log(staticItems.length); // It will still be 3"],[769,"To test code that depends on time, the mocking (substitution) of the functions returning the current time is used — Date.now() or new Date(), for example. In Jest there are jest.useFakeTimers() and jest.setSystemTime() for that. That lets you control the time in tests and make them deterministic. You can also pass the time as a parameter into the function, so that it is easy to substitute it in the tests.","The problem of testing time\\nCode that uses the current time (to calculate discounts, check sessions or for caching, for example) is difficult to test, because the time is constantly changing. Tests can pass in one second and fail in another. The solution is to isolate the dependence on time.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- The inversion of dependencies: pass the time as an argument of the function or through a service that can be substituted in the test.\\n- The mocking of global objects: in a Node.js or browser environment you can substitute Date, setTimeout and others.\\nAn example in Jest\\n\\nWorking with timers\\nTo test setTimeout or setInterval use jest.advanceTimersByTime() or jest.runAllTimers(). That lets you simulate the passage of time without real delays.\\nConclusion\\nThe mocking of time makes tests fast, reliable and independent of real time. This approach is mandatory for unit tests of business logic working with dates and timers.","// The function checking whether a date is today's\\nfunction isToday(date) {\\n  const today = new Date();\\n  return date.toDateString() === today.toDateString();\\n}\\n\\n// The test with the mocking of time\\ntest('isToday returns true for current date', () => {\\n  jest.useFakeTimers();\\n  jest.setSystemTime(new Date('2024-01-15'));\\n  \\n  const result = isToday(new Date('2024-01-15'));\\n  expect(result).toBe(true);\\n  \\n  jest.useRealTimers();\\n});"],[770,"The Application tab in DevTools is used to view and manage all of the data held in the browser: local storage (localStorage), session storage (sessionStorage), cookies, the cache (Cache Storage), IndexedDB, and also to manage Service Workers and PWA manifests. It is a key tool for debugging client-side stores and checking the correct working of a web application.","The purpose of the Application tab\\nThe Application tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) provides a centralised interface for viewing, editing and deleting all of the data a web application saves on the client's side. It is necessary for debugging work with stores, checking the correct setting of cookies, analysing caching and managing Service Workers.\\nThe main sections\\n\\n- Local Storage — data held in the browser with no period of validity, available within one origin.\\n- Session Storage — data that is deleted after the tab is closed.\\n- Cookies — small fragments of data sent with every HTTP request; often used for sessions and tracking.\\n- IndexedDB — a more complex database on the client's side for holding large volumes of structured data.\\n- Cache Storage — part of the Cache API, used to cache resources (in Service Workers, for example).\\n- Service Workers — the management of the background scripts that intercept network requests and implement offline functionality.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you want to check whether the authorisation token is being saved correctly in localStorage. You can open the Application tab, choose Local Storage, find the key 'token' and see its value. You can also change or delete it right in the interface, for testing.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Application tab is an indispensable tool for debugging client-side stores and managing the resources of a PWA. It lets you check and modify data quickly, which speeds up the development and testing of web applications.","// An example of saving a token in localStorage\\nlocalStorage.setItem('token', 'abc123');\\n\\n// The check in DevTools: Application -> Local Storage -> your domain -> token = 'abc123'"],[771,"To emulate different time conditions, fake timers or time mocking are used. That lets you control the time in tests, speeding it up or slowing it down. In Jest, for example, there are jest.useFakeTimers() and jest.advanceTimersByTime(). In Python you can use unittest.mock.patch for time.time().","What is the emulation of time conditions?\\nDuring testing you often have to check the behaviour of code that depends on time: timers, intervals, delays, dates. The emulation of time lets you replace the real time with a controllable one, so that the tests are fast and deterministic and do not depend on the real passage of time.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Fake timers — the replacement of setTimeout, setInterval and Date.now with fictitious versions.\\n- Time mocking — the substitution of the functions returning the time (time(), Date(), for example).\\n- Time travel — the ability to move forwards or backwards in time within a test.\\nAn example in JavaScript (Jest)\\n\\nAn example in Python (unittest)\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe emulation of time is useful when testing caching with a TTL, retries, schedules (cron), animations, timeouts and any functions using setTimeout/setInterval. That makes the tests fast and reliable.\\nConclusion: Use the emulation of time in order to test time dependencies without waiting for real time, which speeds up the execution of the tests and raises their stability.","jest.useFakeTimers();\\n\\nfunction delayedGreeting(name) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(\`Hello, \${name}!\`);\\n  }, 1000);\\n}\\n\\ntest('delayedGreeting logs after 1 second', () => {\\n  const spy = jest.spyOn(console, 'log');\\n  delayedGreeting('Alice');\\n  jest.advanceTimersByTime(1000);\\n  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith('Hello, Alice!');\\n});\\n\\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\\nimport time\\n\\ndef wait_and_return(seconds):\\n    time.sleep(seconds)\\n    return 'done'\\n\\n@patch('time.time')\\n@patch('time.sleep')\\ndef test_wait_and_return(mock_sleep, mock_time):\\n    mock_time.side_effect = [0, 5]  # start time, end time\\n    result = wait_and_return(5)\\n    assert result == 'done'\\n    mock_sleep.assert_called_once_with(5)"],[772,"The Sources tab in Chrome DevTools is used to debug JavaScript code. You can set breakpoints, view and change the values of variables, execute code step by step and analyse the call stack. It is the main tool for finding and correcting errors in client-side code.","The purpose of the Sources tab\\nThe Sources tab in Chrome DevTools is a full environment for debugging JavaScript, CSS and HTML. It lets a developer suspend the execution of code at any moment, investigate the state of the application and execute scripts step by step. It is an indispensable tool for understanding how code works and for finding bugs.\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- Setting breakpoints: You can click on the number of a line in the editor to set a breakpoint. When the execution reaches that line, it will be suspended.\\n- Step-by-step execution: After the stop you can use the buttons Step Over (F10), Step Into (F11), Step Out (Shift+F11) to execute the code one line or function at a time.\\n- Viewing the variables: In the right-hand Scope panel every local, closure and global variable is displayed with its current value. You can change them right during the debugging.\\n- The Call Stack: Shows the chain of function calls that led to the current breakpoint. That helps you understand where the function was called from.\\n- Watch: Lets you add expressions whose values will be computed and displayed at every step of the debugging.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you have a function that does not return the result expected:\\n\\nTo debug it, open the Sources tab, find the file with this code and click on the number of the line \`let total = price + tax;\`. Then execute the code again (reload the page, for example). The execution will stop at that line. In the Scope panel you will see the values \`price = 100\` and \`tax = 20\`. Pressing Step Over, you will move to \`return total;\` and see that \`total = 120\`. If the value had been incorrect, you would have noticed the problem at once.\\nConclusion\\nThe Sources tab is the main tool for debugging front-end code. It lets a developer not simply read logs but investigate the execution of a program interactively, which substantially speeds up the finding and correction of errors. It is worth applying whenever the behaviour of code differs from what is expected, especially in complex scenarios with asynchrony or a multitude of function calls.","function calculateTotal(price, tax) {\\n  let total = price + tax;\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\nlet result = calculateTotal(100, 20);\\nconsole.log(result); // 120 is expected, but there may be an error"],[773,"To set up a front-end build, bundlers such as Webpack, Vite or Parcel are used. The main approaches include code splitting, minification, transpilation through Babel, the management of assets and the configuration of a dev server. Loaders and plugins are also applied to handle various types of file. It matters to configure the development and production modes separately.","The main approaches to setting up a front-end build\\nA front-end build is the process of transforming source code (JS, CSS, images) into an optimised set of files for the browser. The main tools: Webpack, Vite, Parcel, Rollup. Each of them offers its own approaches to configuration.\\nThe key concepts\\n\\n- Entry and Output: the entry point and the folder for the built files.\\n- Loaders: they handle files (babel-loader for JS, css-loader for CSS, for example).\\n- Plugins: they extend the functionality (HtmlWebpackPlugin for generating HTML, for example).\\n- Code Splitting: the division of code into chunks for lazy loading.\\n- Module Federation: lets you load modules from other builds (microfrontends).\\nAn example of a Webpack configuration\\n\\nConclusion\\nSetting up a front-end build is necessary to optimise performance, reduce the size of the bundle and improve the development experience. Use Webpack for complex projects with microfrontends, Vite for fast development, and Parcel for simple configurations without superfluous settings.","const path = require('path');\\nconst HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),\\n    filename: '[name].[contenthash].js',\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      { test: /\\\\.js$/, exclude: /node_modules/, use: 'babel-loader' },\\n      { test: /\\\\.css$/, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },\\n    ],\\n  },\\n  plugins: [new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ template: './src/index.html' })],\\n  optimization: {\\n    splitChunks: { chunks: 'all' },\\n  },\\n};"],[774,"Legacy code is old code that is difficult to maintain and extend. The main strategies: adding tests before changes, gradual refactoring in small steps, the isolation of changes by means of the Strangler Fig pattern, the introduction of automatic checks and the improvement of the documentation. The main thing is not to try to rewrite everything at once but to improve the code iteratively.","What is legacy code and why is it difficult to work with?\\nLegacy code is existing code that often has no tests, is written with obsolete approaches and is poorly documented. The main problem is that any change can break the system, and understanding the logic of its working is difficult because of the tangled dependencies.\\nThe main strategies for working with legacy code\\n\\n- Adding tests (characterization tests): Before changing the code, tests are written that fix the current behaviour. That lets you refactor safely.\\n- Gradual refactoring (baby steps): The changes are brought in in small portions, and every step is accompanied by tests and a check that it works.\\n- The Strangler Fig pattern: The new functionality is written separately, and the old is gradually replaced until it disappears entirely.\\n- The isolation of dependencies: The use of interfaces and dependency injection to reduce the coupling of the code.\\n- The automation of checks: Linters, static analysers and CI/CD help reveal problems at the early stages.\\nAn example: refactoring with tests\\n\\nConclusion\\nWorking with legacy code requires discipline and a gradual approach. The main tools are tests, the isolation of changes and automation. That lets you reduce the risks and gradually improve the quality of the code without stopping development.","// The original legacy code\\nfunction calculatePrice(items) {\\n  let total = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {\\n    total += items[i].price * items[i].quantity;\\n  }\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\n// First we write a characterization test\\nfunction testCalculatePrice() {\\n  const items = [{ price: 10, quantity: 2 }, { price: 5, quantity: 3 }];\\n  console.assert(calculatePrice(items) === 35, 'Test failed');\\n}\\n\\n// Then we refactor\\nfunction calculatePrice(items) {\\n  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);\\n}"],[775,"To structure large front-end applications, a modular architecture, a division into features (feature-based), a layered architecture (Feature-Sliced Design, for example) and microfrontends are used. The main aim is to divide the code into independent, loosely coupled modules, which makes development, testing and maintenance simpler. The choice of approach depends on the scale of the team and of the project.","The main approaches to structuring\\nAs a front-end application grows, a chaotic structure leads to difficulties in maintenance and scaling. There are several proven approaches to organising code.\\nA modular architecture (feature-based)\\nThe code is grouped by functional modules (features) rather than by technical role. The \`auth\` folder, for example, contains its own components, hooks, API requests and styles.\\n\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\nThis is a methodology that divides an application into layers (app, pages, widgets, features, entities, shared) with clear rules of dependency. Every layer may use only those below it.\\n\\nMicrofrontends\\nThe application is broken up into independent parts, which are developed and deployed separately. Every microfrontend can use its own stack. The integration happens through an iframe, Web Components or Module Federation (Webpack 5).\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor small projects a modular architecture is enough. Feature-Sliced Design suits medium and large projects with clear business requirements. Microfrontends are justified in large teams, where the independent development and deployment of parts of the application is required.","src/\\n  features/\\n    auth/\\n      components/\\n      hooks/\\n      api.ts\\n      types.ts\\n    profile/\\n      components/\\n      hooks/\\n      api.ts\\n      types.ts\\n  shared/\\n    ui/\\n    lib/\\n\\nsrc/\\n  app/          // initialisation, routing\\n  pages/        // the pages\\n  widgets/      // self-contained blocks\\n  features/     // the user's scenarios\\n  entities/     // business entities\\n  shared/       // reusable utilities\\n\\n// An example of integration through Module Federation\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  name: \\"app1\\",\\n  remotes: {\\n    app2: \\"app2@http://.../remoteEntry.js\\"\\n  }\\n});"],[776,"Minimising the risks is helped by the isolation of changes through the \\"Strangler Fig\\" pattern, writing tests before edits, the use of feature toggles and gradual refactoring. It matters to introduce changes in small steps, checking each time that the system works. It is also useful to add unit and integration tests for the critical parts of the code.","An understanding of the problem\\nWorking with legacy systems is one of the most difficult tasks in development. The code often has no tests, the documentation is out of date, and the business logic is tangled. Any change can lead to unexpected breakages. The main aim is to bring in changes with a minimal risk of regression.\\nThe main strategies\\n\\n- The Strangler Fig pattern: gradually replace the parts of the old system with new ones, until the old is no longer used. That lets you roll changes back without downtime.\\n- Feature Toggles: introduce new functionality under a flag, so that it can be switched off quickly in the event of problems.\\n- Testing before changes: first write tests on the existing behaviour (characterization tests), then bring in the edits. That creates a \\"safety net\\".\\n- Small steps: make the commits as small as possible, so that it is easy to roll back in the event of an error.\\nA practical example\\nSuppose you have a function for calculating a discount in legacy code:\\n\\nBefore refactoring, write a test fixing the current behaviour:\\n\\nNow you can refactor safely — move the logic out into a config or add new types of user, for example.\\nConclusion\\nApply a combination of isolated changes, testing and gradual refactoring. That lets you modernise a legacy system with a controlled risk and preserve the business's trust.","function calculateDiscount(price, userType) {\\n  // legacy code without tests\\n  if (userType === 'vip') return price * 0.9;\\n  if (userType === 'regular') return price * 0.95;\\n  return price;\\n}\\n\\ntest('calculateDiscount returns correct values', () => {\\n  expect(calculateDiscount(100, 'vip')).toBe(90);\\n  expect(calculateDiscount(100, 'regular')).toBe(95);\\n  expect(calculateDiscount(100, 'guest')).toBe(100);\\n});"],[777,"Gradual refactoring is the improvement of code in small steps without breaking the existing functionality. The main approaches: the Strangler Fig pattern (the gradual replacement of components), Branch by Abstraction (the introduction of an abstraction to replace an implementation), and Feature Toggles (switching between the old and the new version). It matters to have good test coverage and to use the methods of TDD.","What is gradual refactoring?\\nGradual refactoring is the strategy of improving a code base in small, safe steps, without setting aside a separate stage for rewriting the whole project. It lets you develop new features and improve the existing code in parallel, reducing the risks and preserving the team's productivity.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- The Strangler Fig pattern: The gradual replacement of parts of the old system with new ones. The new functionality is written from scratch, and the old code is gradually taken out of service. It suits the replacement of whole modules or services.\\n- Branch by Abstraction: The introduction of an interface or an abstract class, which hides the old and the new implementations. First all of the code is moved over to using the abstraction, then the old implementation is replaced with the new one. Ideal for replacing libraries or internal components.\\n- Feature Toggles: The use of conditional switches to turn new code on and off. It lets you test the new implementation in production on a limited audience and then expand gradually.\\nA practical example (Branch by Abstraction)\\nSuppose we want to replace an old library for working with dates with a new one.\\n\\nConclusion\\nGradual refactoring is a pragmatic approach, which lets you improve code without stopping development. It is worth applying in any project where there is technical debt, in order to avoid the accumulation of problems and preserve the flexibility of the system.","// Step 1: We create the abstraction\\ninterface DateFormatter {\\n  format(date: Date): string;\\n}\\n\\n// Step 2: We wrap the old library\\nclass OldDateFormatter implements DateFormatter {\\n  format(date: Date): string {\\n    return oldLibrary.format(date);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Step 3: We move all of the code over to using the interface\\nfunction displayDate(formatter: DateFormatter, date: Date) {\\n  console.log(formatter.format(date));\\n}\\n\\n// Step 4: We create the new implementation\\nclass NewDateFormatter implements DateFormatter {\\n  format(date: Date): string {\\n    return newLibrary.format(date);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Step 5: We change the implementation in one place\\nconst formatter = new NewDateFormatter();\\ndisplayDate(formatter, new Date());"],[778,"On the front end, unit, integration and end-to-end (e2e) testing are used. Unit testing tests individual functions or components. Integration testing checks the interaction between modules. E2e tests a complete user scenario in the browser.","The main kinds of testing on the front end\\nFront-end testing divides into three main levels: unit, integration and end-to-end (e2e). Each level solves its own task and is applied at different stages of development.\\nUnit testing\\nChecks the working of individual functions, methods or components in isolation. It is tested, for example, whether a component displays the props passed correctly, or whether a date-formatting function works correctly. For that, libraries of the Jest and Vitest kind are used.\\n\\nIntegration testing\\nChecks the interaction of several modules or components. How a form sends data after a button is pressed, for example, or how a list component updates after an element is added. React Testing Library is often used.\\n\\nEnd-to-end testing (E2E)\\nImitates the actions of a real user in the browser: clicks, the entry of text, navigation. It checks that all of the parts of the application work together. The tools: Cypress, Playwright.\\n\\nConclusion: for a high-quality front end you have to combine all three levels. Unit tests give fast feedback, integration ones check the logic of the interaction, and e2e ones guarantee that the user's path works without errors.","// An example of a unit test in Jest\\nimport { formatDate } from './utils';\\n\\ntest('formatDate returns correct string', () => {\\n  expect(formatDate(new Date(2023, 0, 1))).toBe('01.01.2023');\\n});\\n\\n// An example of an integration test with React Testing Library\\nimport { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';\\nimport App from './App';\\n\\ntest('adds item to list', () => {\\n  render(<App />);\\n  fireEvent.click(screen.getByText('Add'));\\n  expect(screen.getByText('New item')).toBeInTheDocument();\\n});\\n\\n// An example of an e2e test in Cypress\\ncy.visit('/login');\\ncy.get('input[name=email]').type('user@example.com');\\ncy.get('input[name=password]').type('password123');\\ncy.get('button[type=submit]').click();\\ncy.url().should('include', '/dashboard');"],[779,"There are several DOM methods for finding elements on a web page. The main ones: \`getElementById\` (to find an element by ID), \`getElementsByClassName\` (to find elements by class), \`getElementsByTagName\` (to find elements by tag) and \`querySelector\`/\`querySelectorAll\` (to find using CSS selectors). These methods let you find one or several elements and interact with them.","JavaScript provides a multitude of methods for finding elements in the DOM, which lets developers interact with a document effectively. Here are the main methods you can use:\\n\\n- \`getElementById(id)\`:\\nThis method looks for an element by its unique identifier (ID) and returns it. If an element with the ID given is not found, it returns \`null\`.\\n\\n- \`getElementsByClassName(className)\`:\\nThis method returns a collection of every element with the class given. Note that the collection returned is live and updates automatically when the DOM changes.\\n\\n- \`getElementsByTagName(tagName)\`:\\nThis method returns a collection of every element with the tag given. The collection returned is also live.\\n\\n- \`querySelector(selector)\`:\\nThis method looks for the first element matching the CSS selector given and returns it. If the element is not found, it returns \`null\`.\\n\\n- \`querySelectorAll(selector)\`:\\nThis method looks for every element matching the CSS selector given and returns a static collection. That is convenient when you have to obtain several elements.\\n\\nEach of these methods has its own peculiarities and advantages, and the choice of the right method depends on the specific requirements of your project. An understanding of these methods lets you manipulate the elements on a web page effectively and build interactive applications.","const element = document.getElementById('myElement');\\n\\nconst items = document.getElementsByClassName('myClass');\\n\\nconst paragraphs = document.getElementsByTagName('p');\\n\\nconst firstItem = document.querySelector('.myClass');\\n\\nconst allItems = document.querySelectorAll('li');"],[780,"When a class component is mounted, the constructor is called first, then render, then componentDidMount. For functional components the order is the same: the body of the function (the analogue of render), then useEffect. On an update, render is called first, then componentDidUpdate or useEffect with dependencies.","The order in which components are called during rendering in React\\nReact renders components recursively, beginning with the root one. For class components the life cycle includes several phases: mounting, updating and unmounting. On mounting the constructor is called first, then render, after which React updates the DOM and calls componentDidMount. For functional components the order is simpler: first the body of the function is carried out (this is the analogue of render), then React updates the DOM, and only after that are the effects from useEffect started.\\nAn example for a class component\\n\\nAn example for a functional component\\n\\nThe order on an update\\nWhen the state or the props change, render (or the body of the function) is called first, then React updates the DOM, and after that componentDidUpdate (for classes) or useEffect with the corresponding dependencies (for functions) is called. It matters to remember that useEffect is carried out asynchronously after the painting, so it does not block the rendering.\\nConclusion: An understanding of the order in which the methods are called helps you avoid errors when working with side effects and optimise performance, especially when using useEffect with dependencies.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    console.log('1. constructor');\\n  }\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    console.log('3. componentDidMount');\\n  }\\n  render() {\\n    console.log('2. render');\\n    return <div>Hello</div>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  console.log('1. render (body)');\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('2. useEffect (after mount)');\\n  }, []);\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}"],[781,"Debounce is a technique that postpones the execution of a function until a particular time has passed since the last call. For a search field that means the request is sent only after the user has stopped typing. That reduces the number of requests to the server and improves performance.","What is debounce and what is it for?\\nDebounce is a technique used to limit the frequency with which a function is called. It guarantees that the function will be carried out only after a particular time has passed since the moment of the last call. That is especially useful for a search field, where every character entered can initiate a request to the server. Without debounce every character pressed would send a request, which leads to a superfluous load and a poor user experience.\\nHow do you implement debounce?\\nThe implementation of debounce involves creating a function that takes another function and a delay. It returns a new function, which on every call resets the timer and sets a new one. When the timer expires, the original function is called.\\n\\nWhere is debounce applied?\\nDebounce is widely used in web development to handle events that can happen often, such as the entry of text, the resizing of the window or scrolling. It helps reduce the load on the browser and the server, improving performance.\\nConclusion\\nDebounce is a simple, effective way of optimising frequent calls of a function. It is worth applying in any scenario where you have to react to user input or to other frequent events, in order to avoid superfluous computations and requests.","function debounce(func, delay) {\\n  let timeoutId;\\n  return function(...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);\\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => func.apply(this, args), delay);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The use for a search field\\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById('search');\\nconst handleSearch = debounce((event) => {\\n  console.log('Search query:', event.target.value);\\n  // Sending the request to the server\\n}, 300);\\n\\nsearchInput.addEventListener('input', handleSearch);"],[782,"To check brackets in a single pass, a stack is used. The opening brackets are placed in the stack, and when a closing bracket is met it is checked whether it corresponds to the top element of the stack. If the stack is empty or the bracket does not correspond, the string is invalid. At the end the stack must be empty.","An explanation of the concept\\nThe bracket-checking task consists in determining whether the brackets in a string are placed correctly. The classic solution uses the stack data structure, which works on the LIFO principle (last in — first out). That lets you check the balance in a single pass over the string, which gives a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n) in the worst case.\\nHow it works\\nThe algorithm goes over every character of the string:\\n\\n- If the character is an opening bracket ('(', '{', '[', for example), it is placed in the stack.\\n- If the character is a closing bracket, it is checked whether the stack is empty and whether the top element of the stack corresponds to that closing bracket. If it does, the top element is removed from the stack. If not, the string is invalid.\\nOnce every character has been handled, the stack must be empty; otherwise the brackets are not balanced.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nThis algorithm is widely used in compilers, interpreters and code editors to check syntactic correctness. It is also basic for an understanding of how a stack works and is often met in interviews.\\nConclusion: Use a stack to check brackets when linear time and simplicity of implementation are required. This approach is optimal for most cases and is easily adapted to different types of bracket.","function isValid(s) {\\n  const stack = [];\\n  const map = { ')': '(', '}': '{', ']': '[' };\\n\\n  for (let char of s) {\\n    if (char === '(' || char === '{' || char === '[') {\\n      stack.push(char);\\n    } else {\\n      if (stack.length === 0 || stack.pop() !== map[char]) {\\n        return false;\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return stack.length === 0;\\n}"],[783,"It is better to carry out filtering at the level of the data rather than in the component. That improves performance, since the component is not repainted unnecessarily, and makes testing simpler. The component should only display the already filtered data.","Where should the filtering of data be carried out?\\nIn a React application it is recommended to carry out the filtering of data at the level of the data (in a Redux store, a server API or a useMemo hook, for example) rather than directly in the component. That follows from the principle of the separation of responsibility: the component is responsible for the display, while the logic of handling the data should be moved out separately.\\nWhy does that matter?\\n\\n- Performance: Filtering in the component can cause superfluous repaints, especially if the data is large or the filter changes often.\\n- Testing: The logic of the filtering is simpler to test in isolation, without a tie to the UI.\\n- Reuse: The filtered data can be used in different components.\\nAn example of bad practice (filtering in the component):\\n\\nAn example of good practice (filtering at the level of the data):\\n\\nConclusion: Filtering should be carried out at the level of the data, in order to preserve performance, make testing simpler and make the code more modular. That is especially relevant for large lists or complex conditions of filtering.","function UserList({ users }) {\\n  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\n  const filteredUsers = users.filter(u => u.name.includes(filter)); // ❌ Filtering in the component\\n  return <div>...</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// In a Redux selector or a hook\\nconst useFilteredUsers = (users, filter) => {\\n  return useMemo(() => users.filter(u => u.name.includes(filter)), [users, filter]);\\n};\\n\\nfunction UserList({ users }) {\\n  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\n  const filteredUsers = useFilteredUsers(users, filter); // ✅ The logic has been moved out\\n  return <div>...</div>;\\n}"],[784,"A search over a tree of data is implemented by means of traversal algorithms: depth-first (DFS) or breadth-first (BFS). DFS uses a stack (recursively or iteratively) and goes to the end of a branch, then returns. BFS uses a queue and traverses the tree level by level. The choice depends on the task: DFS is simpler for deep trees, BFS for finding the nearest node.","The main approaches to searching over a tree\\nA search over a tree of data is the process of finding a node satisfying a given condition in a hierarchical structure. The two main algorithms are depth-first traversal (DFS) and breadth-first traversal (BFS). DFS, recursively or by means of a stack, goes deeper into every branch until it finds the target or reaches a leaf. BFS uses a queue and checks every node of the current level before moving on to the next.\\nAn example of the implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The search for elements in the DOM tree (querySelector uses DFS, for example).\\n- Navigation over a file system.\\n- Algorithms on graphs (social networks, routing).\\nConclusion: DFS is effective for deep trees with limited memory, BFS for finding the nearest node or with an even structure. The choice of algorithm depends on the form of the tree and on the aim of the search.","// The tree: { value: 1, children: [ { value: 2, children: [] }, { value: 3, children: [ { value: 4, children: [] } ] } ] }\\n\\n// DFS (recursively)\\nfunction dfs(node, target) {\\n  if (node.value === target) return node;\\n  for (let child of node.children) {\\n    const result = dfs(child, target);\\n    if (result) return result;\\n  }\\n  return null;\\n}\\n\\n// BFS (iteratively, with a queue)\\nfunction bfs(root, target) {\\n  const queue = [root];\\n  while (queue.length) {\\n    const node = queue.shift();\\n    if (node.value === target) return node;\\n    queue.push(...node.children);\\n  }\\n  return null;\\n}"],[785,"Yes, when a parent node matches, all of its child nodes are usually displayed too, in order to preserve the integrity of the hierarchy. That lets the user see the full context of the element found. The exception is if only an exact match without the nested data is required.","The general concept\\nWhen searching in tree structures (in the DOM tree, a file system or hierarchical menus, for example) the question often arises: do the child nodes have to be shown if the parent matched the search criterion? The answer depends on the context, but in most cases it is yes — the child nodes are displayed, in order to preserve the hierarchy and the context.\\nWhy does that matter?\\nIf the child nodes are hidden when the parent matches, the user can lose their understanding of the structure. In a file manager, for example, when searching for the folder 'Documents' the user expects to see its contents rather than simply an empty folder. In interfaces with trees (a TreeView, for example) that is the standard behaviour.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nThe exceptions\\n\\n- If an exact match without context is required (a search by ID, for example).\\n- In large trees, for performance, you can display only the parent with an indicator of the presence of children.\\nConclusion\\nDisplaying the child nodes when the parent matches improves the user experience and preserves the context. That is standard practice in interfaces with hierarchical data, if there are no special requirements as regards the precision of the search.","// The search function in a tree, with the display of the child nodes\\nfunction searchTree(node, query) {\\n  if (node.name.includes(query)) {\\n    // We show the parent and all of its children\\n    node.visible = true;\\n    node.children.forEach(child => showAllChildren(child));\\n  } else {\\n    // We search in the child nodes\\n    node.children.forEach(child => searchTree(child, query));\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction showAllChildren(node) {\\n  node.visible = true;\\n  node.children.forEach(child => showAllChildren(child));\\n}"],[786,"useEffect is carried out after the component has rendered. On mounting the effect is started; on an update, first the cleanup of the previous effect, then the new effect; on unmounting, only the cleanup. The order of execution depends on the array of dependencies: without it the effect is started after every render, with an empty array only once, on mounting.","The order in which useEffect is carried out\\nThe useEffect hook in React is carried out asynchronously after the component has rendered and the browser has updated the screen. That lets you avoid blocking the user interface while carrying out side effects such as requests to an API or subscriptions to events.\\nThe phases of execution\\n\\n- Mounting: useEffect is started after the first render. If an array of dependencies is passed, the effect is carried out only when those dependencies change.\\n- Updating: When the dependencies change, the cleanup function of the previous effect is carried out first (if there is one), then the new effect.\\n- Unmounting: Only the cleanup function is carried out, without the starting of a new effect.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example, on every press of the button the cleanup is carried out first (if count has changed), then the new effect. On the first render the cleanup is not called.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the order in which useEffect is carried out matters for working correctly with subscriptions, timers and requests. Use the dependencies to control the frequency of execution, and always clean up resources in the returned function, in order to avoid memory leaks.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Example() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Effect runs after render');\\n    return () => {\\n      console.log('Cleanup runs before next effect or unmount');\\n    };\\n  }, [count]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[787,"To check the nesting of brackets, a stack is used. We go over the string: if we meet an opening bracket, we put it in the stack; if a closing one, we check whether it corresponds to the top element of the stack. If the stack is empty or the brackets do not coincide, the string is incorrect. At the end the stack must be empty.","Checking the nesting of brackets by means of a stack\\nA stack is a data structure working on the LIFO principle (last in — first out). It is ideally suited to checking the nesting of brackets, since every opening bracket has to be closed by the corresponding closing one in the right order.\\nThe algorithm\\n\\n- We create an empty stack.\\n- We go over every character of the string.\\n- If the character is an opening bracket ('(', '{', '[', for example), we place it in the stack.\\n- If the character is a closing bracket (')', '}', ']'), we check: if the stack is empty, the string is incorrect; otherwise we extract the top element and compare it with the current bracket. If they do not form a pair, the string is incorrect.\\n- Once every character has been handled, the stack must be empty. If elements remain in the stack, the string is incorrect.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nThis algorithm is used in compilers, interpreters and code editors to check syntax, and also in the parsers of mathematical expressions.\\nConclusion: A stack is a simple, effective way of checking the nesting of brackets. Apply it everywhere you have to be sure of the correctness of paired characters in a string.","function isValidBrackets(s) {\\n  const stack = [];\\n  const pairs = { ')': '(', '}': '{', ']': '[' };\\n  for (let char of s) {\\n    if (char === '(' || char === '{' || char === '[') {\\n      stack.push(char);\\n    } else if (char === ')' || char === '}' || char === ']') {\\n      if (stack.length === 0 || stack.pop() !== pairs[char]) {\\n        return false;\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return stack.length === 0;\\n}\\nconsole.log(isValidBrackets('({[]})')); // true\\nconsole.log(isValidBrackets('({[})'));  // false"],[788,"The traversal of a tree is of two types: depth-first (DFS) and breadth-first (BFS). DFS goes deep along every branch to the end, using a stack (or recursion). BFS traverses level by level, using a queue. DFS suits a search in depth and the checking of connectivity, BFS suits the search for the shortest path in an unweighted graph.","What is the traversal of a tree?\\nThe traversal of a tree is the process of visiting every node exactly once. There are two main approaches: depth-first traversal (DFS) and breadth-first traversal (BFS). The choice depends on the task: to search for an element in a deep hierarchy, for example, DFS is convenient, while to find the shortest path, BFS.\\nDepth-first traversal (DFS)\\nDFS, recursively or by means of a stack, goes deep along every branch. For a binary tree there are three orders: pre-order, in-order and post-order. An example in JavaScript:\\n\\nBreadth-first traversal (BFS)\\nBFS uses a queue and traverses the tree level by level. That is useful for finding the shortest path or for working with graphs. An example:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- DFS — in parsers, serialisation, searching in graphs.\\n- BFS — in shortest-path algorithms, web crawlers, the analysis of social networks.\\nConclusion: DFS is simpler to implement recursively and requires less memory if the tree is deep. BFS guarantees finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph, but it uses more memory on wide trees.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(value) {\\n    this.value = value;\\n    this.left = null;\\n    this.right = null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction dfsPreOrder(node) {\\n  if (!node) return;\\n  console.log(node.value);\\n  dfsPreOrder(node.left);\\n  dfsPreOrder(node.right);\\n}\\n\\nfunction bfs(root) {\\n  const queue = [root];\\n  while (queue.length) {\\n    const node = queue.shift();\\n    console.log(node.value);\\n    if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);\\n    if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);\\n  }\\n}"],[789,"resolve and reject are functions passed into the executor of a Promise. A call of resolve moves the Promise into the fulfilled state with the value passed. A call of reject moves the Promise into the rejected state with an error. Only the first call of resolve or reject has an effect; the subsequent ones are ignored.","A Promise in JavaScript is an object representing the result of an asynchronous operation. It can be in one of three states: pending, fulfilled or rejected. The functions \`resolve\` and \`reject\` are two arguments passed into the executor function when a new Promise is created. They are the only way of changing the state of a Promise from pending to fulfilled or rejected.\\nHow it works\\nWhen you create a Promise, you pass a function with two parameters: \`resolve\` and \`reject\`. A call of \`resolve(value)\` moves the Promise into the fulfilled state, and the value \`value\` becomes the result the \`.then()\` handlers will receive. A call of \`reject(error)\` moves the Promise into the rejected state, and the error \`error\` is passed into \`.catch()\` or the second argument of \`.then()\`.\\nImportant: a Promise can be changed only once. If \`resolve\` is called first, the subsequent calls of \`resolve\` or \`reject\` are ignored. In the same way, if \`reject\` is called first, the remaining calls have no effect. That guarantees that the asynchronous operation will finish exactly once.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example, after 1 second \`resolve\` or \`reject\` is called depending on the condition. The Promise moves into the corresponding state, and the handler needed is called.\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe mechanism of \`resolve\` and \`reject\` is used everywhere you have to work with asynchronous operations: requests to an API, the reading of files, timers, work with databases. It is the foundation for async/await, which makes asynchronous code more readable.\\nConclusion: an understanding of \`resolve\` and \`reject\` is necessary for creating your own Promises and managing asynchronous flows. It is a key element of modern JavaScript, which helps avoid callback hell and makes code more predictable.","const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  // An asynchronous operation, a request to a server for example\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    const success = true;\\n    if (success) {\\n      resolve('The data has been received');\\n    } else {\\n      reject(new Error('An error of the request'));\\n    }\\n  }, 1000);\\n});\\n\\npromise\\n  .then(result => console.log(result)) // 'The data has been received'\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));"],[790,"To change the text content of an HTML element, use the \`textContent\` or \`innerText\` property. If you have an element with the identifier \`myElement\`, for example, you can change its text by means of the following code:\\n\\n\`document.getElementById('myElement').textContent = 'New text';\`\\nThat will update the element's text on the page.","Changing the text content of an HTML element is one of the basic operations when working with the DOM. You can use the \`textContent\` property to set new text without preserving any HTML tags, or \`innerText\` to take the styles of visibility into account. If you have the element \`<p id=\\"myElement\\">Old text</p>\`, for example, you can change its text as follows:\\n\\nThis code will find the element with the identifier \`myElement\` and replace its text with 'New text'. The use of \`textContent\` is preferable when you have to work with text without formatting. If you want to preserve the HTML markup inside the element, you can use \`innerHTML\`, but be careful as regards security, in order to avoid vulnerabilities such as XSS (cross-site scripting).","document.getElementById('myElement').textContent = 'New text';"],[791,"To filter a tree while preserving the parent nodes, a recursive function is used. It checks every node: if it matches the condition, it is included in the result. If not, the function recursively checks the child nodes. If at least one descendant fits, the parent is preserved. That lets you display only the relevant branches without losing the context.","The main concept\\nFiltering a tree while preserving the parent nodes is a task that often arises when working with hierarchical data, such as file systems, navigation menus or organisational structures. The aim consists in filtering the nodes by a particular criterion while preserving all of the parent nodes leading to the filtered elements. That is necessary so that the user sees the full path to the data found and can understand its context.\\nHow it works\\nThe solution is based on the recursive traversal of the tree. For every node we check whether it matches the filtering condition. If it does, the node is included in the result together with all of its descendants (or without them, depending on the requirements). If not, we recursively handle the child nodes. If at least one child node has passed the filter, the current parent node is also included in the result, in order to preserve the structure.\\nAn example of the implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- A search in file managers\\n- The filtering of categories in online shops\\n- Navigation menus with a search\\n- Organisational charts\\nConclusion\\nFiltering a tree while preserving the parent nodes is a powerful tool for working with hierarchical data. It lets users find the information they need quickly without losing sight of its context. This approach is especially useful in interfaces where navigation over nested structures matters, and its implementation requires an understanding of recursion and of working with trees.","function filterTree(tree, predicate) {\\n  return tree.reduce((acc, node) => {\\n    const children = node.children ? filterTree(node.children, predicate) : [];\\n    if (predicate(node) || children.length > 0) {\\n      acc.push({ ...node, children });\\n    }\\n    return acc;\\n  }, []);\\n}\\n\\n// An example of use\\nconst tree = [\\n  { id: 1, name: 'Documents', children: [\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Work', children: [\\n      { id: 3, name: 'Report.pdf' },\\n      { id: 4, name: 'Notes.txt' }\\n    ]},\\n    { id: 5, name: 'Photos', children: [\\n      { id: 6, name: 'Vacation.jpg' }\\n    ]}\\n  ]}\\n];\\n\\nconst filtered = filterTree(tree, node => node.name.includes('Report'));\\nconsole.log(filtered);\\n// Output: [{ id: 1, name: 'Documents', children: [{ id: 2, name: 'Work', children: [{ id: 3, name: 'Report.pdf' }] }] }]"],[792,"There are several approaches to the architecture of front-end applications. MVC (Model-View-Controller) separates the data, the presentation and the control logic. MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) adds a ViewModel layer for binding data. The component approach (React, Vue) builds an interface out of isolated components. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) organises code by business features. Flux/Redux manages state through a one-directional flow of data.","The main approaches to the architecture of front-end applications\\nThe architecture of a front-end application determines how the code is organised, how the components interact with one another and how the state is managed. The choice of approach affects scalability, testability and the speed of development. Let us look at the most popular approaches.\\nMVC (Model-View-Controller)\\nThe classic pattern, separating an application into three parts: the Model (the data and the business logic), the View (the display) and the Controller (the handling of the user's input). Used in Angular and Backbone.js.\\n\\nMVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)\\nAn extension of MVC, where the ViewModel links the View and the Model through the two-way binding of data. Actively used in Knockout.js and Vue.js.\\n\\nThe component approach\\nThe application is built out of isolated, reusable components. Every component contains its own logic, styles and template. Characteristic of React, Vue and Svelte.\\n\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\nA modern methodology, organising code by business features rather than by technical layers. It includes the layers: app, processes, pages, features, entities, shared.\\n\\nFlux/Redux\\nAn architecture with a one-directional flow of data. The state is held in a single store, and changes happen through actions and reducers. It ensures predictability and makes debugging simpler.\\n\\nConclusion: The choice of architecture depends on the scale of the project and of the team. For small applications the component approach will do; for large ones, FSD or Redux. MVC and MVVM remain relevant for frameworks of the Angular kind. The main thing is to observe the principles of the separation of responsibility and the reuse of code.","// An example of MVC in JavaScript\\nclass Model {\\n  constructor() { this.data = []; }\\n  add(item) { this.data.push(item); }\\n}\\nclass View {\\n  render(data) { console.log(data); }\\n}\\nclass Controller {\\n  constructor(model, view) {\\n    this.model = model;\\n    this.view = view;\\n  }\\n  addItem(item) {\\n    this.model.add(item);\\n    this.view.render(this.model.data);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of MVVM with Vue.js\\nnew Vue({\\n  el: '#app',\\n  data: { message: 'Hello' },\\n  methods: {\\n    update() { this.message = 'Updated'; }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// An example of a React component\\nfunction Button({ label, onClick }) {\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;\\n}\\n\\n// The structure of FSD\\nsrc/\\n  features/\\n    auth/\\n      ui/LoginForm.jsx\\n      model/authSlice.js\\n  entities/\\n    user/\\n      api/userApi.js\\n  shared/\\n    ui/Button.jsx\\n\\n// An example of a Redux reducer\\nconst counterReducer = (state = 0, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT': return state + 1;\\n    default: return state;\\n  }\\n};"],[793,"Reflow (relayout) is the process of recomputing the sizes and positions of the elements on a page when the DOM or the CSS changes. Repaint is the updating of the appearance of elements without a change of the geometry. Reflow always causes a repaint, but not the other way round. Frequent reflows reduce performance, so people try to minimise them.","What are reflow and repaint?\\nReflow (or layout) is the process in which the browser recomputes the geometry of the elements: their sizes, positions and margins. It happens when the DOM changes, when CSS properties affecting the sizes change (width, height, margin, padding), or when the window is resized. Repaint is the process of updating the pixels on the screen without a change of the geometry — when the colour of the background or of the text changes, for example.\\nHow are they connected?\\nReflow always causes a repaint, since after the geometry has been recomputed the appearance has to be updated. A repaint can happen without a reflow, if only the visual properties change (color, visibility, background-color).\\nExamples of code\\n\\nHow do you minimise reflow?\\n\\n- Change classes rather than individual styles.\\n- Work with elements out of the flow (position: absolute/fixed).\\n- Use a documentFragment for mass changes of the DOM.\\n- Avoid frequently reading the properties that cause a reflow (offsetHeight, clientWidth).\\nConclusion: an understanding of reflow and repaint helps you write fast web applications, especially with animations and dynamic content. The minimisation of reflow is the key to a smooth interface.","// Causes a reflow and a repaint\\ndocument.getElementById('box').style.width = '200px';\\n\\n// Causes only a repaint\\ndocument.getElementById('box').style.backgroundColor = 'red';\\n\\n// Reading offsetHeight causes a reflow (a forced layout)\\nconst height = document.getElementById('box').offsetHeight;"],[794,"The getBoundingClientRect method returns a DOMRect object with the coordinates of an element relative to the browser's window (the viewport). It contains the properties top, right, bottom, left, width and height. That is useful for determining an element's visibility, for animations or for the positioning of pop-up hints.","What is getBoundingClientRect?\\nThe \`getBoundingClientRect\` method is called on a DOM element and returns a \`DOMRect\` object, which describes the size of the element and its position relative to the visible area of the window (the viewport). The coordinates are counted from the top left corner of the viewport rather than from the document as a whole.\\nThe main properties of the object returned\\n\\n- \`top\` — the distance from the top boundary of the viewport to the top boundary of the element.\\n- \`bottom\` — the distance from the top boundary of the viewport to the bottom boundary of the element.\\n- \`left\` — the distance from the left boundary of the viewport to the left boundary of the element.\\n- \`right\` — the distance from the left boundary of the viewport to the right boundary of the element.\\n- \`width\` and \`height\` — the width and the height of the element, including the padding and the border.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Checking whether an element is visible on the screen (for the lazy loading of images, for example).\\n- The positioning of pop-up windows or tooltips relative to an element.\\n- Animations depending on an element's position (parallax, for example).\\nConclusion\\nThe \`getBoundingClientRect\` method is a simple, effective way of obtaining the precise coordinates of an element in the viewport. It is indispensable for tasks connected with determining visibility and positioning in dynamic interfaces.","const element = document.getElementById('myBox');\\nconst rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();\\nconsole.log(rect.top, rect.left, rect.width, rect.height);\\n// For example: 100, 50, 200, 150"],[795,"Scope determines where variables and functions are available in code. In JavaScript there is global, function and block scope. Variables declared with var have function scope, while let and const have block scope. Nested functions have access to the variables of the outer functions thanks to the lexical environment.","Scope is the execution context in which variables and functions are available to be accessed. In JavaScript scope determines which variables are visible in a particular part of the code, which helps avoid name conflicts and manage memory.\\nThe types of scope\\n\\n- Global scope: variables declared outside any function or block are available throughout the code.\\n- Function scope: variables declared inside a function by means of \`var\` are available only inside that function.\\n- Block scope: variables declared with \`let\` or \`const\` inside a \`{}\` block are available only inside that block.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nThe lexical environment\\nEvery time a function is carried out, a new lexical environment is created, which contains the local variables and a reference to the outer environment. That lets nested functions gain access to the variables of the outer functions — the mechanism called a closure.\\nConclusion: an understanding of scope is necessary for writing predictable, safe code, especially when working with closures, modules and asynchronous operations. The correct use of scope helps you avoid memory leaks and unexpected behaviour of variables.","let globalVar = 'I am global'; // the global scope\\n\\nfunction myFunction() {\\n  var functionVar = 'I am function-scoped'; // the function scope\\n  if (true) {\\n    let blockVar = 'I am block-scoped'; // the block scope\\n    console.log(blockVar); // Available\\n  }\\n  console.log(functionVar); // Available\\n  // console.log(blockVar); // Error: blockVar is not defined\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(globalVar); // Available\\n// console.log(functionVar); // Error: functionVar is not defined"],[796,"No, an animation cannot be made on Promise alone. A Promise manages the order in which asynchronous operations are carried out, but it provides no mechanism for the smooth change of state over time. An animation requires requestAnimationFrame or setInterval, which cause the frame to be updated. A Promise can be used to organise a sequence of animations, but not for the rendering itself.","Why a Promise is unsuitable for an animation\\nA Promise is an object representing the result of an asynchronous operation. It lets you carry out code after a task has finished, but it provides no means for controlling the time or the frequency of the updates. An animation requires an element's properties (its position or opacity, for example) to be changed at a particular frequency (usually 60 frames per second). A Promise cannot generate such repeating calls.\\nHow a Promise can be used in an animation\\nA Promise can be useful for organising a sequence of animations. You can create a function that returns a Promise resolving after one animation step has finished, for example. Then, by means of a chain of .then(), you can start the next step. The animation step itself has to be implemented by means of requestAnimationFrame or setInterval, however.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nA Promise cannot be the only tool for an animation, since it does not control the frames. In combination with requestAnimationFrame, however, it lets you create complex sequences of animations with clear control over the order of execution.","function animateElement(element, targetPosition, duration) {\\n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\\n    const start = performance.now();\\n    const startPosition = element.offsetLeft;\\n\\n    function step(currentTime) {\\n      const elapsed = currentTime - start;\\n      const progress = Math.min(elapsed / duration, 1);\\n      const currentPosition = startPosition + (targetPosition - startPosition) * progress;\\n      element.style.left = currentPosition + 'px';\\n\\n      if (progress < 1) {\\n        requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n      } else {\\n        resolve();\\n      }\\n    }\\n\\n    requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst box = document.getElementById('box');\\nanimateElement(box, 300, 1000)\\n  .then(() => animateElement(box, 100, 500));"],[797,"The recomputation of the layout (reflow) happens when the geometric properties of elements change: width, height, position, margins, or when elements are added to or removed from the DOM. A reflow is also caused when the window is resized, when CSS pseudo-classes are activated (\`:hover\`, for example), when certain properties are read (offsetHeight, scrollTop) and when the content changes (the text, for example). It is an expensive operation, so people try to minimise it.","When does the recomputation of the layout (reflow) happen?\\nThe recomputation of the layout (also called reflow) is the stage of rendering at which the browser computes the geometry (the sizes and positions) of every element on the page. It happens every time properties affecting an element's size or position change.\\nThe main causes of a reflow:\\n\\n- A change of the geometric CSS properties: width, height, margin, padding, border, top, left, display (a change to block/inline-block), position.\\n- The addition or removal of elements from the DOM.\\n- A change of an element's content (the replacement of text or of an image, for example).\\n- The activation of CSS pseudo-classes such as :hover, :focus.\\n- A change of the size of the browser's window (resize).\\n- The reading of certain properties, which force the browser to carry out a reflow: offsetHeight, offsetWidth, scrollTop, scrollHeight, clientTop, getComputedStyle() and others.\\nAn example of code causing a reflow:\\n\\nHow do you minimise a reflow?\\n\\n- Group the changes of styles (use classList.add or cssText).\\n- Work with elements out of the flow (position: absolute/fixed).\\n- Use a documentFragment for the mass addition of elements.\\n- Avoid reading the properties that cause a reflow in loops.\\nConclusion: An understanding of reflow matters for optimising the performance of web pages, especially with animations and the dynamic updating of content. The minimisation of the recomputations of the layout speeds up the rendering and improves the user experience.","const el = document.getElementById('box');\\nel.style.width = '200px'; // a change of the geometry -> a reflow\\nconst height = el.offsetHeight; // a forced read -> a reflow\\nel.style.height = '300px'; // another reflow"],[798,"In JavaScript this is equal to undefined in strict mode ('use strict') if a function is called without an explicit context. For example, on a simple call of a function or in a callback passed without a binding. In non-strict mode this in such a case refers to the global object (window in the browser).","When is this equal to undefined?\\nThe value of \`this\` in JavaScript is determined by how the function is called rather than by where it is declared. \`this\` becomes \`undefined\` in strict mode (\`'use strict'\`) when a function is called without an explicit context — that is, not as a method of an object, not through \`call\`/\`apply\`/\`bind\` and not as a constructor.\\nThe main cases\\n\\n- A simple call of a function: if a function is called on its own, \`this\` will be \`undefined\` in strict mode.\\n- Callbacks: when a function is passed as a callback (into \`setTimeout\` or an event handler, for example), the context is lost unless it is bound.\\n- Inside nested functions: even if the outer function has a context, an inner function (declared through \`function\`) receives its own \`this\`, which in strict mode will be \`undefined\`.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nHow to avoid losing the context\\n\\n- Use \`bind\`, \`call\`, \`apply\` for an explicit binding.\\n- Use arrow functions — they do not create their own \`this\` but inherit it from the outer lexical environment.\\n- Preserve the reference to \`this\` through a variable (\`const self = this\`, for example).\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of when \`this\` is equal to \`undefined\` is critical for avoiding errors in asynchronous code and in callbacks. In modern JavaScript it is recommended to use arrow functions or an explicit binding of the context, in order to guarantee predictable behaviour.","'use strict';\\n\\nfunction showThis() {\\n  console.log(this); // undefined\\n}\\n\\nshowThis();\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  method: showThis\\n};\\n\\nobj.method(); // obj (there is a context)\\n\\nconst callback = obj.method;\\ncallback(); // undefined (the context has been lost)"],[799,"Rendering is blocked by \`<script>\` without attributes and \`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\`. The browser stops building the DOM until it has loaded and carried out the script or applied the styles. In React applications this topic matters when setting up \`index.html\`, SSR/SSG and lazy loading.","What «blocking the rendering» means\\nThe browser builds the DOM sequentially, from top to bottom. If it meets a tag requiring a resource to be loaded or carried out, it stops and waits. The page is not displayed until that resource has been handled.\\n\`<script>\` — blocks the parsing of the DOM\\n\\nThe difference between \`async\` and \`defer\`:\\n\\n- \`async\` — is carried out at the moment it loads, and the order is not guaranteed\\n\\n- \`defer\` — is carried out after the parsing, in the order of declaration\\n\\nReact applications (CRA, Vite) add \`defer\` to the bundle by default.\\n\`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\` — blocks the rendering\\n\\nCSS blocks the rendering, because the browser has to build the CSSOM before painting — otherwise the page would «flash» without styles (FOUC).\\nWhat does NOT block the rendering\\n\\nHow it looks in practice\\n\\nThe React specifics\\nIn React these tags are rarely worked with directly, but the topic comes up in several contexts:","<!-- ❌ Blocks: the browser will stop the parsing until it has loaded and carried it out -->\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- ✅ async: loads in parallel, carries out immediately after loading -->\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- ✅ defer: loads in parallel, carries out after the full parsing of the DOM -->\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- ❌ Blocks: the browser will not paint a single pixel without the CSS -->\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n\\n<!-- ✅ The loading of non-critical CSS without blocking -->\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"print.css\\" media=\\"print\\">\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"heavy.css\\" media=\\"(min-width: 1200px)\\">\\n\\n<!-- Images — they do not block, they load in parallel -->\\n<img src=\\"photo.jpg\\">\\n\\n<!-- Fonts through font-face — they do not block the parsing, but they can cause FOIT -->\\n<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"font.woff2\\" as=\\"font\\" crossorigin>\\n\\n<!-- preload/prefetch — only hints to the browser, they do not block -->\\n<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"chunk.js\\" as=\\"script\\">\\n<link rel=\\"prefetch\\" href=\\"next-page.js\\">\\n\\n<head>\\n  <!-- ❌ Blocking CSS — normal for the critical styles -->\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"critical.css\\">\\n\\n  <!-- ✅ Non-critical CSS — asynchronous loading through the onload trick -->\\n  <link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"non-critical.css\\" as=\\"style\\"\\n        onload=\\"this.rel='stylesheet'\\">\\n</head>\\n\\n<body>\\n  <!-- the content -->\\n\\n  <!-- ✅ Scripts at the end of the body or with defer — the DOM has already been built -->\\n  <script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n</body>\\n\\n// Code splitting — Webpack/Vite will add defer automatically\\nconst LazyPage = React.lazy(() => import('./Page'));\\n\\n// Next.js — built-in management of critical CSS\\n// The components' styles are inlined into the <head>, the rest is deferred\\n\\n// Vite/CRA index.html — the bundle always goes with defer\\n// <script type=\\"module\\" src=\\"/src/main.jsx\\"><\/script>\\n// type=\\"module\\" behaves like defer by default"],[800,"Time to Interactive (TTI) is a performance metric that shows how many seconds after the beginning of the loading a page becomes fully interactive. It takes into account the time at which the content has been displayed, the main scripts have been carried out and the user can interact with the elements without delays. TTI helps you assess how quickly a user can start working with a page.","What is Time to Interactive (TTI)?\\nTime to Interactive (TTI) is a performance metric of a web page, which measures the time from the beginning of the navigation to the moment the page becomes fully interactive. Full interactivity means that the content has been displayed, the main scripts have been loaded and carried out, and the user can interact with the elements (press buttons or fill in forms, for example) without noticeable delays.\\nHow is TTI measured?\\nTTI is computed on the basis of two key moments:\\n\\n- The page must have been visually displayed (First Contentful Paint).\\n- The browser's main thread must be free of long tasks for at least 5 seconds.\\nLong tasks are tasks running for longer than 50 ms, which block interaction. TTI is fixed after the network and the main thread have stabilised.\\nAn example of code for tracking TTI\\nAlthough TTI is usually measured by tools of the Lighthouse kind, you can assess it approximately by means of a Performance Observer:\\n\\nWhere is TTI applied?\\nTTI is critically important for the user experience, especially on mobile devices and with slow connections. A low TTI means the user can start interacting quickly, which improves engagement and conversion. The metric is used in performance audits (Lighthouse, WebPageTest) and to optimise the loading of scripts, reduce the size of the JavaScript and eliminate long tasks.\\nConclusion\\nTTI helps developers assess how quickly a page becomes ready for interaction. Apply this metric to optimise the critical rendering path, especially on sites with a large quantity of JavaScript, in order to ensure a smooth user experience.","const observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.name === 'first-contentful-paint') {\\n      console.log('FCP:', entry.startTime);\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'paint', buffered: true });\\n\\n// TTI requires more complex logic, usually through libraries"]]`);export{e as default};